# Peasant draw (played as "The peasant's game"): an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 5, 6, 7], "peasant-draw", "Unlucky peasants rematch",
      "Four players · twelve years · the same brutal seed · an agreed stop",
      "The Emperor and the three peasants, no Warlord, and the same deck that ruined the last peasant table. This time they buy land from the year it opens, "
      "and the throne goes from Reina to Silke, from Silke to Graham, and back to Reina. But the land never catches up with the mouths, "
      "and nothing is built after Year 5. When the population doubles again in Year 11 the crown falls deep into debt. The table votes three times on stopping where it sits. "
      "Silke says no until she's back in the seat she drew, and after Year 12 all four agree.",
      short="4 players · 12 years")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Four players: the Emperor and the three peasants (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Warlord, so nobody raids and no soldiers are sold. No Official, Gentry or Scholar, so nobody collects rent, and the Emperor appoints a banker for the Harvest Pile, a tax collector and someone to read I Ching and count the population, at the Scholar's salary of 5 (Note 3). Nothing is owed to Provinces 2, 3 or 4. Each realia province holds one piece of its own realia and two of the one type it never needs. With no upper class at the table, the Silk Peasant stands in for it: a Silk Peasant who completes the objective may take the throne (Section 11).")
act("Four booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Graham Garner the Wheat Peasant.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
deal_grain("Reina sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Silke, Paddy and Graham take their realia out of the box: one piece of their own and two of the type they'll never need. It waits beside the board until it's sold.")
act("Graham cuts out the Province 7 Reservoir, two squares by two, and sets it aside until it's built.", map_hl=[7])
act("Reina hands out the table jobs of the empty seats for the whole game: Graham Garner banks the Harvest Pile and pays out every harvest, Paddy Doyle collects the tax, and Silke Weber reads the I Ching card each year and keeps the population counter, for the Scholar's 5 a year from the crown.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Right, everyone gets a job, so nobody can say the crown played favourites. Graham, you hold the Harvest Pile, since wheat people count carefully. Paddy, you collect the tax, and please be gentle with your neighbours. Silke, you read the I Ching, and the crown pays you five a year to tell us honestly what the heavens say.",
 "Reina: The coast is where a revolt would start, so the only paid post goes to Silke and the tax goes through Paddy's hands, which ties both coastal provinces to the throne. Graham sits inland and gets the bank, which gives him a reason to keep the pile honest and the books quiet.")
act("Silke shuffles the I Ching deck and sets it face down. The first card is turned in Year 2.", map_hl=[5])

# ===== CHAPTER 1: You Already Had a Brush
# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Reina", "Forty percent this year, friends. That's twenty-four from each of you, and you still keep more than you hand over. The crown pays for the Summer Palace on its own, and next year we start on the Wall and the canals.",
 "Reina: The tax brings in 72, Silke's salary costs 5 and the palace costs 20, so the crown ends the year at 107 with the Wall and the Province 1 canal now open to build. I'm taxing firmly while incomes are positive and building every year, and I'm keeping a reserve back for the day the population doubles and the land starts costing money.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G20", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G20, in Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20. 500 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Each seller holds two types beside the board and chooses which goes up: Silke the Dragon Jar, the only one in the game, minimum 4; Paddy one of his two Bamboo Brushes, minimum 4; Graham one of his two Land Deed Scrolls, minimum 2. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Silke", "Thank you, Your Majesty. Five a year to read the heavens is fair, and I'll read them honestly, good news or bad. The Dragon Jar goes up first, opening at four. It's a lovely piece, and nobody at this table has much money, so I'm not going to pretend it's worth forty.",
 "Silke: I sell the Dragon Jar first because nobody's set seems to need it, so I won't be handing anyone a piece of the throne. I'm keeping the Customs Houses back until I can see who wants them badly, and my grain is going on a Brush and a Bowl when Paddy's lots come up.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Reina, I'll be gentle with the collecting, I promise. Right then, one Bamboo Brush from the rice country, starting at four. It's a good honest brush, and I've got another just like it for next year, so nobody needs to go mad over it.",
 "Paddy: I've got two brushes and only the one bowl, so the brush goes first and the bowl stays scarce for when somebody really wants it. I need every grain for the Jar, the Vase and the rest, so I'm watching what Silke and Graham chase before I spend a thing.")
say("Graham", "One Land Deed Scroll, opening at two. I've got two of them, so whoever wants paper on the land had better start now.",
 "Graham: I've got two scrolls and only one vase, so the scrolls have to start going first or I'll still be stuck with one in year three. Starting at two costs me nothing, and I want to see who at this table bids on deeds before any new land opens.")
act("The Storyteller tells Silke that Paddy's and Graham's sets both need the Dragon Jar.", map_hl=[5])
say("Silke", "Well, I was wrong about the Dragon Jar. Paddy, Graham, it turns out you both need it and there's only the one, so it opens at four and the two of you can sort it out between you. I'll take a polite interest in the brush and the scroll, and I won't be emptying my purse on either.",
 "Silke: I've just learned my jar is the one piece both my rivals can't do without, so I keep it at four and let them outbid each other while I collect a quarter of the price. I'm bidding modestly on the brush and the scroll because there's a second of each next year, and if those two wear each other down over the jar, both will come cheap.")
lot(5, "DJ", [
 B(6, 14, "Graham, I won't pretend I don't want that Jar, because I do, and so do you. If we fight over it, the Harvest Pile gets fat and we both go hungry. Still, there's only the one, so I'll bid what's sensible.",
   "Paddy: There's only one Dragon Jar in the whole game, and Graham needs it the same as I do, so I'll stretch for it, but I'm not emptying the sack. Graham's got two Land Deeds, so if I miss the first one cheap there's another next year."),
 B(7, 16, "Only one Dragon Jar in the whole game, so I'll bid on it, and I won't be shy about it.",
   "Graham: There's only one jar, and whoever doesn't get it can never finish a set, so I'll go further for it than for anything else."),
 B(6, 17),
 B(7, None, "Fair enough. I'll stop there, I've counted what I can afford.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham went to 16 to keep the Jar from Paddy; Paddy would have gone to 18 to keep it from Graham."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on R22, in the middle of the paddies.")), at="R22")
say("Paddy", "Seventeen. That's more grain than I like to spend on anything I can't eat, but there was only the one. Sorry, Graham, you pushed me right to the edge there. She goes in the middle of the paddies where I can keep an eye on her.",
 "Paddy: That's the only Dragon Jar in the game, so now Graham can't finish his set without coming to me first. I've got 19 grain left, which is thin, and I lost the scroll to Silke on a tie, so next year I'll have to be cleverer about the second one.")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 4, "Paddy, you've got two brushes. If one ends up in Silke's hands this year, that's fine by me, there's another one next spring.",
   "Graham: The brush isn't worth a fight, because Paddy has to put the second one up next year anyway."),
 B(5, 5),
 B(7, None, "Fair enough. I'll stop there, I've counted what I can afford.")],
 hover="Silke puts the Brush on P25, at the far end of the coast.", at="P25")
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(6, 6), B(5, 6),
 B(6, None, "Fair enough, it's yours. I'm not paying more than the pot's worth to me.")],
 hover="Both limits stood at six, and a tie goes to the earlier seat: Silke, in Province 5, before Paddy in 6. Silke puts the Scroll on P26, beside her Brush.", at="P26")
say("Silke", "A brush and a deed for eleven, and four back from my Jar. Thank you, gentlemen. Paddy, enjoy the Jar. Seventeen is a lot of rice to hand over for one pot.",
 "Silke: That's two of my four pieces for a net seven, while Paddy and Graham paid thirty-three between them to fight over a jar. I've put mine at the far end of the coast, out of the way of any canal the crown wants to dig, and I'm watching Paddy now, because he has a Bowl and the Jar.")
salaries({5:5}, text="Reina pays Silke 5 for reading the I Ching, the Scholar's salary.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "The canal gives way", "canal")
act("Silke turns the first I Ching card: The Canal Gives Way. An average harvest for every province. No canal has been dug, so none is lost. Provinces 2, 3, 7 and 8 play a personal disaster; 2, 3 and 8 are empty, so that's Graham. The population holds at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[7])
say("Silke", "The heavens say: 'The Canal Gives Way. The engineers blame the river. The river declines to comment.' Average harvest in every province. Any unfinished Grand Canal work is lost, and since nobody has dug a canal yet, the crown loses nothing. Provinces 2, 3, 7 and 8 play a Personal Disaster, so Graham, I'm sorry, but that's you. The population holds at one hundred million, times one, so read the first row of your tables.",
 "Silke: Graham turns his disaster card while I sit on a brush and a deed with a quiet harvest coming. I need the Bowl from Paddy and the Vase from Graham, and Graham with a disaster on top of him might sell cheaper than he'd like.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(7, "a late frost blackens the edge of the wheat.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent again, which comes to fourteen each on a thin year. Graham, I'm sorry about the frost. The crown digs the whole northern canal this year, both sections in one go, so the river has nothing half-finished to swallow. Your Reservoir is on the list, and it's coming.",
 "Reina: That card wipes out half-dug canals, so I'm building both sections at once for 80; the canal pays the crown 20 a year from now on and has paid for itself in four. After the tax, Silke's salary and the canal I'm down to about 79, and that's still a cushion for when the land opens.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={1:2}, text="Public works: Reina digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1 in one year, one project. They add 20 to her harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 80. 420 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Silke has only Customs Houses left, so one goes up; Paddy puts up his second Bamboo Brush, minimum 4, and keeps the Wooden Bowl back; Graham puts up his last Land Deed Scroll, minimum 5, and keeps the Vase back.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "Second brush, as promised, Graham, same as the first, opening at four. And I'm sorry about the frost. Losing two to a cold night is hard on anybody.",
 "Paddy: Graham bid on the last brush and lost it, so I reckon he'll want this one, and the quarter I keep helps fill my sack back up. The Wooden Bowl's the only one in the game, so I'm holding it back until someone really needs it.")
say("Graham", "The other Land Deed Scroll. Minimum five. Paddy, you went to six for the last one.",
 "Graham: Paddy has the only jar now, so I can't finish a set unless that changes, and I've written that down. Until then I sell to whoever's shown they'll pay: Paddy and Silke both went to six on the first scroll, so I'm setting the floor just under that.")
say("Silke", "One Customs House, opening at three. It's good for trade and a quiet port. As for the brush and the scroll, I already have mine, gentlemen, so I'm only watching. Mostly.",
 "Silke: These are the last brush and the last scroll in the game, and Graham needs one while Paddy needs the other. If I buy both, I'm the only peasant who can still finish a set. Their Bowl and Vase still have to go up for auction whether they like it or not, so all I have to do is keep enough grain to win them.")
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(6, 4, "Graham, there are two Customs Houses and two of us after them. Let's not feed the Harvest Pile. You take this one, I'll only put in a small bid, and next year you leave the second one to me. That's fair, isn't it?",
   "Paddy: I'll go high for it and keep the Customs House cheap, and if Graham shakes on the deal I get the second one next year without a fight."),
 B(7, 5, "Paddy. Silke has two Customs Houses, and you and I need one each. I won't bid against you on this one if you leave the next one to me. No point feeding the Pile.",
   "Graham: Two Customs Houses and two buyers is just arithmetic, so there's no reason for a war over them."),
 B(6, None, "No, that's past what it's worth to me. Go on, it's yours.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Each offered the other this one and the next, and both bid anyway: Graham's limit was 6, Paddy's 4."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on Q15.")), at="Q15")
say("Graham", "Q15. Thank you, Paddy, the next one's yours, as agreed.")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 5, "The brush I'll take at Paddy's price.",
   "Graham: The brush nobody else needs should come to me cheap. I can't finish without the jar, and I know it, but I'm still collecting pieces in case the jar ever moves."),
 B(5, 6),
 B(7, None, "No. I've counted it.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke already holds a Brush; she bid only against Graham, and would have gone to 13 to keep this one from him."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on P24, beside the other one.")), at="P24")
say("Graham", "Silke. You already had a brush.",
 "Graham: Silke paid six for a brush she didn't need, only so I couldn't have it. Paddy has the jar and she has both brushes now, so I've lost two pieces I needed to people who say they're only watching, and I'm noting every one of them.")
say("Silke", "Two brushes on my coast, so I'll be the best-lettered peasant in China. Graham, I'm sorry, but six is cheap for a good brush, and you agreed it was Paddy's price.",
 "Silke: Graham can't finish a set now, because every brush in the game is on my shore. Paddy outbid me for the scroll, so he's the one I race from here: I need his Bowl and Graham's Vase, and I have to keep my grain dry for both.")
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(6, 5, "And yes, I'll be bidding on that last scroll, I won't pretend otherwise.",
   "Paddy: That's the last Land Deed in the game and I'm the only one who still needs it, so it's the piece I can't lose."),
 B(5, 12), B(6, 13),
 B(5, None)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke already holds a Scroll; she bid only against Paddy, up to 12. Paddy would have gone to 15."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on R23, beside the Jar.")), at="R23")
say("Paddy", "Thirteen, and Silke, you made me earn every grain of it, for a scroll you already had. Graham, we crossed our wires on who got which Customs House, but you've got this one now, so by my reckoning the last one's mine next year and you'll stand aside. I'll hold you to that, same as I'd hold myself.",
 "Paddy: The scroll goes right beside the Jar, so that's two of my four, and both were the only ones left. All I need now is that last Customs House and Graham's Vase, and Silke bidding twelve on a scroll she didn't need tells me she's watching me now.")
salaries({5:5}, text="Reina pays Silke 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: The Mandate Moves to the Coast
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "The villages grow", "half2")
act("Silke turns the second I Ching card: Population Grows by Half. A good harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population rises to 150 million, x1.5, and every province eats half as much again.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Silke", "\"Population Grows by Half. The villages are noisier than they used to be.\" Good harvest everywhere, no disasters named. The counter goes to 150 million, so that's times one and a half, everyone. Read the x1.5 row, and remember your mouths just got hungrier too. Honest reading, as promised.",
 "Silke: One more doubling and the new land opens at twenty a square, and I'm the one sitting on fifty-five grain while Paddy scrapes along at twenty-nine. My Customs House goes on sale this year, and a bigger crowd makes it worth more, so I'll price it accordingly.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "A good year, and a good year is when the crown asks for more. Fifty percent, which is twenty a head, and you each still clear twenty. Graham, I said the Reservoir was coming, and it's coming now. The crown puts in forty if you put in twenty, and it pays you twenty a year from next harvest, so your share is back in your pocket after one year. Silke, your five, as always.",
 "Reina: Bad years bring in nothing, so I'm taxing the fat one at fifty and spending it on the promise I made Graham before he starts to doubt it. With his twenty in the split I keep the reserve near a hundred and twenty-four, which is the money I'll need when the land opens at times two.")
taxes(.50, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
say("Graham", "Twenty in, twenty back each year from next harvest. That pays for itself in one year, Reina, and I don't argue with a number like that. Build it.",
 "Graham: Twenty grain buys me twenty a year for as long as the game runs, which beats anything going at auction. The Vase goes up later and I'll want every grain the other two have left to be bid against each other, not spent on me.")
build("Province 7 Reservoir", 60, {1:40, 7:20}, key="res7", at="O16:P17", text="Public works: Reina and Graham build the Province 7 Reservoir, two squares by two, on O16 to P17, the crown paying 40 and Graham 20. It adds 20 to Graham's harvest from next year.", hover="The 60 goes into the Harvest Pile. 360 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Each seller has one piece left, so each goes up: Silke's last Customs House, minimum 15; Paddy's Wooden Bowl, minimum 4; Graham's Vase, minimum 8. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Graham", "I said I'd stand aside on the Customs House, so I'm standing aside. Paddy, it's yours to win. The Bowl I'll bid on, and the Vase opens at eight. There's only one, and two of you need it.",
 "Graham: The Bowl's no use to me unless someone trades me a brush or the jar, but if I hold it, Silke's set stops with me, and that's the first leverage I've had in this game. The Vase at eight: one of them will pay it, and the more they bid against each other, the more my quarter comes to.")
say("Paddy", "Graham and I shook on this Customs House last year, and I'm grateful he's keeping to it. Silke, I'll not pretend I don't want your Vase too, Graham, so I'll bid it honestly and no higher than I can stand. And the Bowl goes up at four. It's a good bowl, and I'll not give it away.",
 "Paddy: The House should come cheap if Graham keeps his word, and he always has. Then everything I've got goes on the Vase, as long as I still have twenty left over to finish the set, because if Silke gets that Vase she's one step from me, and I can't have that.")
say("Silke", "My last Customs House starts at fifteen, Paddy. It's the only one left in China, and you know exactly what it's worth to you. As for the pottery, I'm only bidding to keep the kiln-men honest.",
 "Silke: Whatever Paddy pays for my Customs House he can't spend on the Vase, and I get a quarter of it back for my trouble. If I get the Bowl and the Vase this year, I hold four of the five with grain enough to finish, and the reader of cards turns into the one who picks the next Emperor.")
act("Silke's Customs House opens at 15. Paddy, the only bidder, would go to 8, so nobody meets the minimum: it stays beside the board and must go up again next year at 2.", map_hl=[5,6])
lot(6, "WB", [
 B(7, 8), B(5, 9), B(7, 12), B(5, 13),
 B(7, None, "That's past what it's worth to me. Take it.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham would have gone to 12 only to keep the Bowl from Silke; Silke would have gone to 24 to keep it from Graham."),
          ("Silke", "She puts the Bowl on P23.")), at="P23")
lot(7, "VA", [
 B(6, 20), B(5, 21), B(6, 24), B(5, 25),
 B(6, None, "That's more than this farm can spare. Fair bidding to you.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy went to 24 to keep the Vase from Silke; Silke would have gone to 40 to keep it from Paddy."),
          ("Silke", "She puts the Vase on P22, beside the Bowl.")), at="P22")
say("Silke", "A bowl and a vase on the coast, next to my brushes and my deed. Thank you, Paddy, and thank you, Graham, both very fair prices. I'm only putting them on the shelf. For now.",
 "Silke: Brush, scroll, bowl and vase all sit in a row on my coast, and I still have thirty-seven grain, well past the twenty I need, so the set is complete. The Customs House I couldn't sell may be my last real bargaining chip, and now everyone at this table is wondering whose seat I'll take.")
swap(5, 1, "Silke Weber declares a complete set (Bamboo Brush, Land Deed Scroll, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 37 grain, and takes the throne: with no upper class at this table, the Silk Peasant may. Silke becomes Emperor of Province 1; Reina Osorio becomes Silk Peasant of Province 5. A new Emperor returns all her realia: both Bamboo Brushes and the Wooden Bowl go back to Province 6's stock, the Land Deed Scroll and the Vase to Province 7's, to be auctioned again. Silke takes the crown's reserve of 129; Reina draws 60 from the Harvest Pile. The unsold Customs House stays in Province 5's stock, now Reina's to sell.",
 hover="Silke: That's a hundred and sixty-six grain and the whole empire, and every piece goes back to the stock to be sold again, so the race starts over with me as the one to beat. Reina knows the throne better than anyone, so she's the one I'll be watching from up here.")
say("Silke", "Brush, scroll, bowl and vase, and grain to spare. Reina, the cards have been reading honestly all along, and this year they say the Mandate moves to the coast. I take the throne. Don't worry, the I Ching still needs a reader, and I'll pay a fair five for one.")
act("Silke hands out the table jobs as the new Emperor: Graham Garner keeps the Harvest Pile and now also reads the I Ching and keeps the population counter, at 5 a year; Paddy Doyle keeps collecting the tax. Reina holds no job.", map_hl=[1,6,7])
say("Silke", "Graham keeps the Harvest Pile and takes the cards, at five a year, the same as I got. Paddy keeps the tax. Reina, rest those hands for a year, you've earned it. I'll run an honest crown, but not a soft one.",
 "Silke: Graham can't build a set any time soon, so the jobs and the five grain go to the one friend who has nothing to gain from my fall. Reina and Paddy are the ones who'll be racing for those pieces again, and I'd rather not hand either of them a crown salary to bid with.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Silke pays Graham 5 for reading the I Ching from next year.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "The seed rotted", "dribble2")
act("Graham turns the third I Ching card: Bad Harvest. A bad harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population holds at 150 million, x1.5.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.\" The counter stays at 150 million, so it's still times one and a half, everyone. Read the bad-harvest row at x1.5. No disasters named. And Your Majesty, a bad year is a poor year for fifty percent.",
 "Graham: Silke paid twenty-five for my Vase, and a turn later it's back in my stock to sell again, which is the best piece of luck I've had. The tax is what I'm watching now: we get a thin harvest this year, and our new Emperor just found out what the throne costs to keep.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it.")
say("Silke", "Graham's right, a rotten year is no year to squeeze anyone. Thirty percent, and only Graham owes it, three grain. The crown pays for the Winter Palace itself, and that opens the way for canals on the coast, Reina's and Paddy's.",
 "Silke: A light tax costs me almost nothing, and a twenty-grain palace that unlocks their canals gives the two hungry ones a reason to want me to stay. I'm keeping the reserve deep, because when the population doubles I'll have to buy land, and they'll have to buy it too.")
taxes(.30, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H21", text="Public works: Silke builds the Winter Palace on H21, in Province 1. It must stand before the canals in Provinces 5 and 6.", hover="Silke pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 340 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Reina's Customs House went unsold last year, so it must go up again at 2. Paddy puts up the Wooden Bowl, minimum 6, and keeps both Bamboo Brushes back; Graham puts up the Vase, minimum 10, and keeps the Land Deed Scroll back.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "The Bowl's come home to me again, so up it goes. Six to start. Graham went to twelve for it last year, so I don't think six is asking much. And Reina, I'm sorry about the throne. That Customs House of yours at two is the first good news I've had in a year.",
 "Paddy: Graham and Reina both fancy that Bowl now, and every quarter-share I get from the sale goes toward the Vase. Silke beat me by a single grain last year, and I won't let it happen twice.")
say("Graham", "The Vase again. It's still the only one in China, and last year it went for twenty-five. It opens at ten.",
 "Graham: Paddy needs it and so does Reina's new seat, and both of them have short purses after a rotten year, so ten is a floor they can reach but not one they'll get it under. I'll keep the Scroll back for next year, so I've always got something they need.")
say("Reina", "Well. Silke played the long game and played it beautifully, and I'll say so on the record. The coast is mine now, so I'm starting over like everyone else: my Customs House is up at two, cheap, and I'll be bidding on the pottery, but nobody's going to see me burn my grain to get it.",
 "Reina: I've lost the throne and I'm down to fifty, so I'm doing what Silke did: get hold of the scarce piece, which is the one Vase in China, and only pay for the Bowl if it comes cheap. Whatever happens I keep twenty-plus back, because the set means nothing without the grain to claim with.")
say("Paddy", "Reina, I'll take that Customs House off your hands, and gladly. Graham, ten's a fair start for the Vase. I'll say it plainly to everyone: if I get the House and the Vase both, I've got my set, and I'll still have my twenty grain kept by.",
 "Paddy: The House should come cheap because I'm the only one who needs it, so this year it comes down to the Vase. I'll go as high as I can and still keep the twenty grain the set needs, and not a single grain past that.")
say("Graham", "The Customs House is Paddy's business, not mine. I'll bid for the Bowl, though, because I need it, and I won't lose it by one grain twice.",
 "Graham: The brushes are back in Paddy's stock now, so my set's alive again if I take this Bowl. And Reina can't finish hers without it, so I'll go a little further to keep it off the coast.")
lot(5, "CH", [B(6, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Paddy; his limit was 10."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on R24, beside the Scroll.")), at="R24")
lot(6, "WB", [
 B(5, 6), B(7, 7), B(5, 8), B(7, 9),
 B(5, None, "Take it, it's yours. I'll be back for the next one.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina stopped at 8; Graham would have gone to 12, or 15 to keep the Bowl from her."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on Q16, beside his Customs House.")), at="Q16")
lot(7, "VA", [
 B(6, 10), B(5, 11), B(6, 21), B(5, 22),
 B(6, 22.5, "Twenty-two and a half, and that's every grain I can spare and still keep my twenty. I won't pretend I've got more, Reina. If you go past it, it's yours, and I'll shake your hand.",
   "Paddy: If I win at that, I'm left with exactly twenty grain and holding the full set, Jar, Deed, House and Vase. Reina can outbid me if she wants it badly enough, but it'll cost her nearly half of what she's got."),
 B(5, 23.5, "Paddy, you told the whole table that the Vase finishes your set, and I've just come off the throne, so I know exactly what that sentence means. Sorry, the Vase is coming to the coast.",
   "Reina: Paddy has to keep twenty in hand to declare, so anything much over twenty-two is out of his reach as a set-maker, and twenty-six makes sure he can't get there. It costs me some grain, but it stops a second coup this year and gives me the one piece nobody else can get."),
 B(6, None, "That's past what the paddies can bear. Well bid, and good luck to you with it.")],
 hover=TH(("", "A close lot: Reina's limit was 22 and Paddy's 21, so each had one raise, Reina to 26 and Paddy to 22.5."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on P22, where Silke had it.")), at="P22")
say("Paddy", "Right next to the Deed, there. Well played, Reina, I said I'd shake your hand and I will. But the Vase only has to come back round once, and when it does, I'll be the one waiting for it.",
 "Paddy: That's three of my four pieces sitting in a row at R22 to R24. Reina's got the Vase now, but she has nothing else, so if she climbs before I do, every piece goes back to stock and the Vase comes back up for auction.")
say("Graham", "Nine. I lost by one grain last year, so it's a pleasure to win by one this time. It goes next to my Customs House. Paddy, those two brushes in your stock: I'll be bidding on one when it comes up.",
 "Graham: Customs House and Bowl now, so I need a brush from Paddy's stock and that Jar. And Reina's paid twenty-three and a half for a Vase with no Bowl to go beside it, so she's stuck until I sell or she buys mine.")
say("Reina", "Back on the shelf where Silke had it, P22. Paddy, I'll take that handshake, and I mean it. You bid honestly and you stopped where you said you'd stop.",
 "Reina: That leaves me with twenty-seven and the only Vase in China, so whoever wants the throne next goes through the coast. Now I need the scroll, the brush and the bowl, and I need to rebuild my grain while I collect them.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Silke pays Graham 5 for reading the I Ching, as she promised.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: Every Grain into the Ground
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "The villages are noisier", "half1")
act("Graham turns the fourth I Ching card: Population Grows by Half. The population has already grown by half once, so only the good harvest counts: a good harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population holds at 150 million, x1.5.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "\"Population Grows by Half.\" It has already grown by half once, so that part doesn't count. Only the good harvest does. The counter stays at 150 million, times one and a half. Everyone reads the good-harvest column on the x1.5 row. Nobody plays a disaster this year.",
 "Graham: A good year with my Reservoir under it means the most grain I've had yet. The land is still shut until the population doubles, so I'm keeping it all together, and I'll want to see how much Paddy is asking for a brush.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Silke", "A good harvest is a good year for everyone, the throne included. Forty percent, and in return the capital gets a Pagoda you can see from the coast. Reina, Paddy, look out your windows: that's your tax standing up in marble.",
 "Silke: Fifty-six in, twenty out for the cheapest work on the list, and Graham's five for the cards. The reserve goes up to about a hundred and eighty, which is what I'll need the day the population doubles and I have to buy land square by square. Graham owes the most this year and he's the one I'm paying, so watch whether he grumbles.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
build("Marble Pagoda", 20, {1:20}, key="Marble Pagoda", at="G22", text="Public works: Silke builds the Marble Pagoda on G22, in Province 1. It may only be built in a year when nothing else is, and it is the year's only project.", hover="Silke pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 320 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Paddy and Graham each hold only one type beside the board: Paddy puts up a Bamboo Brush, minimum 2, and Graham the Land Deed Scroll, minimum 6. Reina has nothing left in stock. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Forty in a good year, Silke, and a Pagoda besides. I'll take that, and I'll hold you to the coast canal next. Graham, nobody else at this table needs that scroll, so let's not feed the Harvest Pile for nothing: open it at the minimum and it's mine for a few grain. Paddy, you've got two brushes, so there's one for each of us if Graham and I don't drive the first one up.",
 "Reina: Fifty-one grain, twenty held back for the claim, so I can spend about thirty this year on a scroll and a brush together. The scroll should go cheap because I'm the only one who needs it, but I'm watching Graham on the brush, because he owns the Bowl I still need, and I don't want him buying the same things I'm buying.")
say("Paddy", "Just the one Bamboo Brush from me, at two, and somebody's welcome to it. The Deed's no use to me, I've got one already on my paddies, so I'll keep my hands in my pockets for that one, Graham.",
 "Paddy: I'm not saying what anything's worth to me this year, not after last time. Every grain I don't spend on a lot I don't need goes towards the Vase and the twenty I have to hold back, and that's all I'm thinking about.")
say("Graham", "There are two brushes, Reina, so neither of us has to bleed for the first one. I'll go to a fair price and stop there. The Scroll opens at six. That's less than I paid to stand aside for Paddy's Customs House.",
 "Graham: Fourteen is my number. Past that the second brush comes cheaper next year, and three quarters of anything I overpay goes into the Pile. Reina is the one to watch: she has fifty-one grain, and she needs a brush as much as I do.")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 2), B(5, 6), B(7, 10), B(5, 12), B(7, 13),
 B(5, None, "Go on then. Paddy has a second brush, and I can wait a year.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina stopped at 12; Graham would have gone to 14."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on Q17, beside the Bowl.")), at="Q17")
lot(7, "LD", [B(5, 6)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Reina; her limit was 8."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on P23, beside the Vase.")), at="P23")
say("Graham", "Thirteen, one more than you, Reina. Paddy still has a brush in his stock, and it'll come cheaper than mine did. Enjoy the Scroll.",
 "Graham: That's three of the four: the Customs House, the Bowl and now the Brush, all in a row on the Q line. All I lack is the Dragon Jar, and Paddy is the only one who has one, so I'll be reminding him who stood aside for his Customs House.")
say("Reina", "Thank you, Graham. Six was a fair opening and I won't forget it. The deed goes next to the Vase, so the coast has two pieces now. Enjoy the brush, and save the second one for me, Paddy.",
 "Reina: That's two of four for forty-five grain left, and it means the Bowl in Graham's hands is the piece I'll have to buy, so I'll keep things friendly with him. He outbid me by one grain for the brush, so he's counting my shelf the same way I failed to count Silke's.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Silke pays Graham 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "Fat years make large families", "double1")
act("Graham turns the fifth I Ching card: Population Doubles. A good harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population doubles for the first time, from 150 million to 300 million, x3, and new land opens: 20 a square, yielding 20 a year from next year.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "\"Population Doubles. Fat years make large families.\" A good harvest in every province, but the counter goes to 300 million. That's times three now, so read the times-three row. Everyone's consumption doubles from here. Feed them while you still can.",
 "Graham: Six more mouths to the square and land still at twenty. This is the year I've been saving for, and I'll be buying squares before the other two have finished their sums.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it.")
say("Silke", "Three hundred million mouths, and not one province with a grain of income to tax. So no marble this year. Every grain the crown has goes into the ground. I'll pay five on every square any of you buys, and I'm buying four for the north myself. Fill your fields first, then come and ask me for canals. Reina, the coast canal is still coming. It comes once there's a harvest to pay for it.",
 "Silke: A square pays twenty a year and a canal section pays ten for forty, so the Wall and the Canal can wait until somebody here has a taxable income again. Four squares gets the north close to even and leaves me about twenty in reserve for a bad harvest. Reina's down to twenty-five and holds the Vase and the Scroll, so she's the one I'm watching while everyone else plants.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Public works: Silke builds nothing this year. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed.", map_hl=[1])
buy_land(1, ["F23", "F24", "F25", "F26"], {1:80}, "New land: Province 1 buys four squares of sea, F23 to F26, for 80, paid by the crown into the Harvest Pile. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
say("Reina", "Silke, five a square is a kind offer to someone who has the grain. I have twenty-five. Here's every grain I can put in the ground, twenty for two squares, and I'm asking the crown to match it, ten a square. Three hundred million mouths on the coast is your problem as much as mine, and an empty coast doesn't pay for canals.",
 "Reina: Two squares cuts my loss from forty-five a year to five. That keeps me out of debt, which means I can still buy and the coast doesn't revolt on Silke's watch. I'm asking her for twice what she offered, because if she won't feed the coast she'll be in trouble long before I am.")
say("Silke", "Eight a square, Reina. That's sixteen from the crown and twenty-four from you, and you've got twenty-five, so both squares go in the ground. I'm not tipping my last grain into the sea when Paddy and Graham haven't asked yet and Graham still needs paying for the cards. The coast gets its fields and the crown keeps its feet.",
 "Silke: Ten a square would bring me down to fifteen before Graham's five and the others' shares, and a broke crown can't pay anyone's share next year, Reina's included. At eight she still gets both squares, so nobody can call it a refusal, and the four grain I keep might be what keeps me out of debt.")
say("Reina", "Eight a square, done. Both squares go in the ground, and the coast keeps its feet as well, Silke. That's one grain left in my purse, so nobody at this table should be afraid of me this year.",
 "Reina: With both squares my average year goes from losing forty-five to losing five, so I'm close enough to even that one decent harvest gets me back on my feet. Being down to one grain also tells the table I'm no threat for the throne, and that's useful while I wait for Paddy's brush.")
buy_land(5, ["N26", "N27"], {5:24, 1:16}, "New land: Province 5 buys two squares of sea, N26 and N27, for 40: Reina pays 24 and the crown 16, 8 a square. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
say("Paddy", "Three squares for the south-east, Silke. That's forty-two from me and six a square from the crown, and I know what's left in your chest after Graham's paid, so I'm not asking for Reina's eight. Three hundred million mouths won't eat pots, and I'd rather owe nobody than sit on grain while my paddies go hungry.",
 "Paddy: Three squares is what it takes to stop losing forty-five grain a year, and if the game ends next year they still pay back more than I put in. It leaves me well short of the Vase for now, but a man in debt can't buy anything, and I don't fancy being that man.")
say("Silke", "Five a square, Paddy, as I offered at the start. You can cover forty-five and still have grain in your pocket, and Reina couldn't. Once Graham's paid there's barely a handful left in the chest, and he hasn't asked for his share yet. I won't leave the south centre with nothing just to give the south-east one more grain a square.",
 "Silke: At six a square Graham's land would get nothing at all, and refusing a province its share is how revolts start. Paddy can pay the extra three grain easily, so he can grumble about it. He's got no grounds to march.")
say("Paddy", "Five a square it is, Silke, and fair enough. Graham's got to eat as well. Three squares go into the south-east, and I'll be back to four and a half grain, same as when I started out.",
 "Paddy: Three squares is exactly what I need to stop the paddies losing grain every year, so I'd sooner be poor and steady than keep nineteen and watch it drain away. The Vase can wait a bit longer. It'll come round again.")
buy_land(6, ["R25", "R26", "S26"], {6:45, 1:15}, "New land: Province 6 buys three squares of sea, R25, R26 and S26, for 60: Paddy pays 45 and the crown 15, 5 a square. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
say("Graham", "Four squares for the south centre, Silke. One a square from the crown, four in all, and that still leaves your chest enough for my five. I've done the sums. I don't need your eight, and I'm not leaving you short to pay me.",
 "Graham: At three hundred million I'm twenty-five short a year, and four squares make that a profit of fifty-five, with twenty-seven and a half still in my purse for the Jar. I asked the crown for its last four grain so that when Silke's chest is empty, nobody can say I left her owing.")
buy_land(7, ["T14", "T15", "T16", "T17"], {7:76, 1:4}, "New land: Province 7 buys four squares of the Warlord's land, T14 to T17, for 80: Graham pays 76 and the crown 4, 1 a square. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
act("The auctions. Paddy holds only one type beside the board, so his last Bamboo Brush goes up, minimum 2. Reina and Graham have nothing left in stock. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "One grain in my purse and the minimum is two, so the brush is safe from me this year, Paddy. Keep it on your shelf if nobody bids. I'll be good for it once the coast fields come in.",
 "Reina: Every grain I had went into land, so I can't bid for the brush. The only other player who'd want it is Graham, and he already has one. With luck it stays unsold until I can afford it next year.")
say("Paddy", "Two grain, and not a grain more, so it's open to anybody who wants it this year. If nobody does, Reina, it'll be on my shelf waiting for you once the coast comes in.",
 "Paddy: Nobody at this table has grain to spare, so I'd be daft to ask for more than the minimum. If it sells I'll get half a grain, and if it doesn't I've lost nothing, and I'll have a buyer next year.")
say("Graham", "Pass. I've got a brush, and I know what it looks like when someone buys a second one just to keep it from somebody. I won't do that. Paddy, the piece I'm waiting on is your Jar. I stood aside for your Customs House, and when you're ready to talk about that Jar, I'll be the one at this table with grain.",
 "Graham: Reina can't reach two grain, so a bid from me would only have bought a brush I don't need, and three quarters of it would have gone into the pile. My twenty-seven and a half is for the Jar, and with the whole table broke, I'm the only buyer Paddy's going to find.")
act("Paddy's Bamboo Brush opens at 2, and nobody bids: Reina has 1 grain, and Graham already holds a brush. It stays beside the board and must go up again next year at 2.", map_hl=[6])
salaries({7:5}, text="Silke pays Graham 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary. It takes the crown's last 5.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: Wheat to Silk to the Throne
# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "The ox went lame", "dribble3")
act("Graham turns the sixth I Ching card: Bad Harvest. A bad harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population holds at 300 million, x3. This year's incomes: Silke -30, Reina -30, Paddy -10, Graham +30. Before the harvest is paid out, the table may give or lend grain.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame.\" All provinces collect a bad harvest. Population stays at three hundred million, so read the x3 row, bad-harvest column. Nobody draws a disaster. Silke, my five's due at the end of the year, same as always.",
 "Graham: A bad year at x3 is what I bought those four squares for. Everyone else ends this year in debt, and I'm the one who'll still have grain when Paddy decides what his Jar is worth.")
say("Silke", "Another rotten year, and the crown has no shelf left to sell. You all know I put every grain into your fields. Graham, lend the crown thirty now and I'll pay you back thirty-five from the first tax I raise, with your five on top as always. A crown in debt can't pay its share of a single square next year, and that hurts every one of you, not just me. Reina, Paddy: if you have to sell, sell the pieces you need least. The Vase and the Jar are the last things I'd let go to the Pile.",
 "Silke: I'm out of grain and out of pieces, so the only thing I have left to trade is my word, and Graham is the only one at this table with grain to lend. If he says yes, the crown stays out of debt and can still buy land. If he says no, everyone gets to watch the Empress go broke, and I'll make sure they remember who let it happen.")
say("Reina", "Graham, you're the only granary at this table, so I'll be plain. The Pile pays two for the only Vase in China, and I won't hand it over for two. Twenty-nine buys it outright, or lend me twenty-nine and keep it as your pledge. Either way the Vase stays out of Paddy's hands, and that's worth more to you than whatever Silke's paying in interest. Silke, I'm not selling my land to save a piece. I'll be here next year to pay my share of the square.",
 "Reina: One grain against a thirty loss means the Vase goes to the Pile for two and I'm left in debt and can't bid. Selling it to Graham for twenty-nine keeps me solvent and keeps Paddy one piece short. It's my set, I know. But a woman in debt can't make it back to the throne anyway, and it doesn't matter what's on her shelf.")
say("Paddy", "Graham, I'll be straight with you, same as I was over the Customs House. I'm five and a half short, and the Pile would take three of my pieces for it. You paid thirteen for the last Brush, so this one's yours for six, right now, and nobody owes anybody. Reina, I'm sorry, I did say it'd be on the shelf for you, but you've one grain and I've a set to keep. If you'd sooner lend me six, Graham, I'll pay it back from the first harvest the new fields bring in, every grain.",
 "Paddy: If the Pile gets my Jar and the Deed for two apiece, that's thirty years of saving gone for six grain, so the Brush has to go, even though I half promised it. Graham's the only one at the table with grain this year. Everyone wants something off him, so I'm offering him the cheapest deal going.")
act("The Storyteller rules that Paddy's Brush can't be sold privately: it is unsold stock beside the board, and unsold stock is sold only at auction, so it goes up this year at 2. Paddy's other offer, a loan of 6 from Graham, stands.", map_hl=[6])
say("Graham", "Paddy, I won't lend you six. I'll give you ten for the Jar, today. You end the year with grain, you keep your Customs House and your Scroll, and I'm the one who stood aside for that Customs House when it counted. Silke, Reina, I'm sorry. I can do one thing this year and I've picked it.",
 "Graham: Ten for the Jar leaves me twenty-two and a half, and twenty of that is what the set costs, so for the first time I can finish it. The Pile would give Paddy two for that Jar; I'm offering five times that, and he knows the arithmetic as well as I do.")
say("Paddy", "Done, Graham. Ten for the Jar, and I'll shake on it. It's not what it cost me, but you stood aside for me once and you're the only one who's put grain on the table today, so I won't haggle with a man for that. Look after it on Q18. If I ever come asking for it back, I'll be paying properly.",
 "Paddy: It hurts to let the Jar go for ten after all those years, but if I'd said no, the Pile would have taken all three pieces for six grain, and this way I keep the Scroll and the Customs House and I don't owe anybody. Now I need the Jar and the Vase both, and I know exactly who's holding each one.")
give(pid(6), pid(7), "DJ", at="Q18")
act("Graham buys Paddy's Dragon Jar for 10, before the harvest. The Jar moves from R22 to Q18, beside Graham's Customs House, Bowl and Brush: the Wheat Peasant's set is complete.", "A trade of a piece on the board, at any time (Section 5).", {pid(7): -10, pid(6): 10}, map_hl=[6,7], deal=True)
sell_back(pid(5), ["VA", "LD"], "Reina can't cover her -30 from the 1 she holds, so before the harvest is paid both her pieces go back to the Harvest Pile at 2 each: the Vase from P22 and the Land Deed Scroll from P23. They go into the pile's own stock, which it auctions after the provinces' lots.", "Sell before you sink: a payment that would take a player below zero is first covered by selling realia on the board back at 2 a piece; only the shortfall left becomes a debt.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it. Silke and Reina can't cover theirs: the crown ends at -30 and Reina at -25, both in debt.")
say("Graham", "The set's complete, so I'll use it. Reina, I'm taking the Silk seat. Your twenty-five of debt comes off my grain, not yours, and you start over with sixty and four squares of good wheat land that pay fifty-five a year. Silke, I'm one step closer to you now. I'll be watching the coast.",
 "Graham: Seat five beats seat six, and my seat at the end is the only number that decides it, so I give up the Jar and twenty-five grain to move two places up. I bought that Jar for ten, so it's still cheap, and a Silk set is the one road left to the throne.")
swap(7, 5, "Graham completes the Wheat set and replaces Reina as the Silk Peasant, in Province 5. He takes Reina's -25 with the seat, leaving him 27.5; the Dragon Jar goes back to Province 5's stock, and his Customs House, Bowl and Brush move to P22, P23 and P21. Reina draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Wheat Peasant, with Province 7: the Reservoir and four squares of new land.",
 "A complete set and 20 grain in hand let a peasant replace another peasant (Section 11). The mover takes the displaced player's grain, debt and all; the displaced player draws 60.", at={"CH":"P22", "WB":"P23", "BB":"P21"})
say("Silke", "Ninety percent, and I won't pretend that's gentle. Reina, you're the only one with a harvest to tax, so it comes out of your thirty: twenty-seven grain, and every grain of it pays off the crown's debt, none of it goes on palaces. It still leaves the crown three short. While I'm in the red I can't pay a share of anyone's field, so the fastest way back to land for all of us is to get me out of debt this year, not in three. Graham, your five waits until I'm back above zero. You could have spared me thirty today and picked a seat instead, so you'll understand. Reina, when the crown can pay again, your squares are the first I put grain into.",
 "Silke: Graham turned down my loan, bought the Jar and took a seat, and now he holds three pieces and has said out loud he's coming for me. I'm not paying my rival's wages from an empty chest. Reina gets hit hard this year, but she's the one I'll court next year, and I'd rather she owe me a field than have Graham owe me nothing.")
taxes(.90, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Public works and new land: the crown is still in debt (-3), so it can't pay a share of any work or any square. Nothing is built, and no province can buy new land this year. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed.", map_hl=[1])
act("The auctions. Graham, now the Silk Peasant, puts up the Dragon Jar from Province 5's stock, minimum 8. Paddy's last Bamboo Brush went unsold last year, so it must go up at 2. The Harvest Pile auctions the two pieces Reina sold back, the Vase and the Land Deed Scroll, at 2 each. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Graham", "The Jar goes up at eight. Reina, it's the one piece you need that nobody else at this table can use, and you've got the grain. I'm passing on the Brush. I'll bid on what the Pile has, and I won't pretend I'm not.",
 "Graham: I need the Vase and the Scroll, and only Paddy wants the Vase too, with four and a half grain. If I keep the two of them near ten I can make up the set's twenty out of the Jar money and Silke's back salary, and after that only the crown is left above me.")
say("Paddy", "The Jar's past my purse, and good luck to whoever takes it home. Reina, the Brush is on the block at two, like I promised; it's not quite my shelf any more, but it's waiting for you all the same. I'll have a go at the Vase with what I've got, and if Graham beats me to it a third time, I'll shake his hand, same as I did the first two.",
 "Paddy: Four and a half grain won't win much against Graham's twenty-seven, but if nobody bids on the Vase, four might just bring it home. I'm keeping half a grain back so I'm not left with nothing.")
say("Reina", "Graham, that was clean, and I've been on the other end of a clean move before. I'll take the sixty and the wheat. The Jar comes to Province 7 at Graham's eight. Paddy, I'll take that Brush at two the way you promised, and I'll remember you kept the shelf for me. The Vase I'm not after, but Graham, don't count on getting it cheap.",
 "Reina: Paddy's got four and a half and Silke's in debt, so nobody can reach Graham's eight on the Jar except me. That means two pieces of a new set for about ten grain, and I keep twenty back for next year. The six on the Vase is just to make Graham pay for his next step, because I won't go into a bidding war with the man who has my old seat.")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(7, 8)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Reina; her limit was 10."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on Q15.")), at="Q15")
lot(6, "BB", [B(7, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Reina; her limit was 4."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on Q16, beside the Jar.")), at="Q16")
say("Reina", "The Jar and the Brush are going in the middle of the wheat, where everyone can see them. Paddy, you kept your word on the Brush, and I'll keep mine to you. Two down, Silke. I'm counting shelves this time, mine included.",
 "Reina: That's half a new set and twenty-three grain left, for ten grain. Now I need a Customs House and a Bowl, and both of them are sitting with Paddy and Graham, so next year it's all about trading.")
lot(PILE, "VA", [
 B(6, 4), B(6, None, "Go on, then. It was never going to be cheap, that Vase."),
 B(7, 6), B(7, None, "Take it, and well bought."),
 B(5, 7)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy stopped at 4; Reina bid only to keep it from Graham, up to 6. Graham would have gone to 10."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on P24.")), at="P24")
lot(PILE, "LD", [B(5, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Graham; his limit was 7."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on P25, beside the Vase.")), at="P25")
say("Graham", "That's the Bowl, the Brush, the Vase and the Scroll, and twenty in hand. Silke, I'm taking the throne. The crown's three short and I'll cover it; you draw sixty, which is more than the crown has had all year. Paddy, Reina: the tax will be what I can show you the numbers for.",
 "Graham: Seven years ago Silke paid six for a brush she didn't need to keep me off a set, and today I finished a better one and took her chair for seventeen and a half. One more project to build, and the throne is where I want to be standing when the last one goes up.")
swap(5, 1, "Graham completes the Silk set (Bowl, Brush, Vase, Scroll) with 20.5 in hand and takes the throne. Every piece he held goes back to stock. He takes the crown's reserve as it stands, -3, leaving him 17.5, and the crown is out of debt. Silke draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Silk Peasant, with Province 5.",
 "At a table with no upper class the Silk Peasant may take the throne with a complete set and 20 grain in hand. Becoming Emperor returns every piece; the old Emperor hands over the reserve, debt and all, and draws 60.")
say("Graham", "Paddy, you keep the tax. You collected it honestly and you'll get two for it tonight. Reina, the bank and the I Ching are yours from next year, three a year each, and I'll pay it on time. Silke, you've sixty in hand, which is more than the crown has, so you'll forgive me for not paying you anything.",
 "Graham: Fifteen and a half in the reserve and three hundred and twenty still owed on works, so every grain I hand out tonight has to buy me goodwill. Silke paid six once for a brush she didn't need, and tonight I needed to pay her nothing at all.")
act("Graham hands out the table jobs as the new Emperor: Paddy Doyle keeps collecting the tax; Reina Osorio banks the Harvest Pile and reads the I Ching from next year, at 3 a year for each job. Silke holds no job.", map_hl=[1,6,7])
salaries({6:2}, text="Graham pays Paddy 2 for collecting this year's tax.", hover="An appointee is paid only if the Emperor chooses. The crown ends the year at 15.5.")

# ============================== YEAR 8 ==============================
R(8, "The Emperor's share", "share")
act("Reina turns the seventh I Ching card: The Emperor's Share. An average harvest for every province, and no disasters; every province with a public work splits that work's extra harvest 50/50 with the Emperor this year. The population holds at 300 million, x3.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "The Emperor's Share. 'The Emperor would like to remind you who paid for the ditch.' Every province gets an average harvest. Any province with a Public Works splits the extra harvest it brings in fifty-fifty with the Emperor this round, and that includes my Reservoir, which I seem to have paid for twice now. Population holds at three hundred million, times three, so read the x3 row, average column, and nobody plays a disaster. And Graham, I don't mean any disrespect, but the book says the card reader gets at least five, not three. I'll take five at the end of the year, the same as Silke paid it and I paid it.",
 "Reina: I built that Reservoir with the crown's grain, and now the crown takes half of what it brings in, so I'm making sure the salary is right in the same breath. With twenty-three in hand and Jar and Brush on the shelf, all I need is Paddy's Bowl and somebody's Customs House, so this year I'm being nice to Paddy and Silke.")
act("The Storyteller notes: an appointed reader's salary is the Emperor's choice; the 'at least 5' is a seated Scholar's due.", map_hl=[1,7])
harvest("Reina pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it.")
act("The Emperor's Share: the Province 7 Reservoir adds 20 to Province 7's harvest, so Reina pays the Emperor half of it, 10.", "Only Province 7 has a public work outside the crown's own province; the canals in Provinces 5 and 6 aren't built.", {pid(7): -10, pid(1): 10}, map_hl=[1,7])
say("Graham", "Thirty percent. That's twenty-one, and it's less than anyone here has paid under the last crown. Silke, one canal section in your province, twenty from you and twenty from me, and it's plus ten on your harvest every year after. Reina, it's three, as I said when I gave you the job. I don't pay more than I said, and I don't pay less.",
 "Graham: The crown goes to forty-one and a half, spends twenty on the canal and still has enough left for wages, and a modest tax is the cheapest protection against a revolt I can buy. The canal also gets Silke putting grain into my works instead of saving it against me.")
taxes(.30, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
say("Silke", "Graham, you're asking me to pay twenty for a canal that earns me ten a year, when twenty on a square of land earns me twenty. The canal is the crown's work, so build it with the crown's grain: I'll put in ten and you put in thirty. If not, leave it unbuilt, because every year that canal stays unbuilt is another year this game keeps going. I'll take two squares, K25 and K26, and I'm asking the crown for ten on each. Two a square isn't a subsidy, it's a tip. I ran this crown with an empty chest and still paid five a square, and you're sitting on forty-one. Everyone here can do that sum.",
 "Silke: Land pays double what the canal does, so my grain goes into fields. The rest stays in my purse, because Paddy has the Bowl and the Brush and Reina has the Vase and the Scroll, and they'll come to auction. The longer that canal stays unfinished, the more years I get to rebuild a set and take my throne back, and if Graham keeps the land subsidy this stingy, he's giving me the case for a revolt himself.")
say("Paddy", "One square, Graham, Q26, right next to the paddies. I'll put in fifteen of my seventeen and a half and ask the crown for five, the same five a square Silke paid when the chest was empty. I'm not asking for a tip or a gift, just what the last crown managed.",
 "Paddy: Two a square would leave me half a grain short, so I'm asking for five, and if he holds at two I'll just about scrape it. A square that pays twenty every year it runs on is worth more to me than seventeen grain sitting about waiting for a Jar that Reina has no reason to sell.")
say("Reina", "No land for the wheat this year. Province 7 already pays fifty-five, and I'd rather the crown's grain go to the people who need it to break even. Graham, Paddy's asking for five a square, the same five Silke found with an empty chest, so give it to him. And three for the cards is your call, so I'll take it and say thank you.",
 "Reina: I've got fifty-one and a half, and I need twenty kept back to claim, plus enough to win the Bowl and a Customs House at auction. A square of land pays me back one time if the Wall and the Canal finish the game next year, but a set puts me in a seat. So I'm keeping every grain for the shelf and letting Silke and Paddy spend theirs on dirt.")
say("Graham", "Five a square, for everyone, which is what the last crown paid and what Paddy asked for. Silke, when I bought those four squares of wheat land I asked for one a square so you could still pay me, so I won't be told about tips. The canal waits until somebody thinks it's worth more than ten of their grain.",
 "Graham: Five on each square takes away anyone's excuse for a revolt and still leaves the crown twenty-six and a half. If the canal stays unbuilt it's because Silke wouldn't pay her twenty, and the whole table heard her say so.")
act("Public works: Graham builds nothing this year; Silke would pay only 10 of the Province 5 canal section, and he wouldn't pay 30. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed.", map_hl=[1,5])
buy_land(5, ["K25", "K26"], {5:30, 1:10}, "New land: Province 5 buys two squares of sea, K25 and K26, for 40: Silke pays 30 and the crown 10, 5 a square. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
buy_land(6, ["Q26"], {6:15, 1:5}, "New land: Province 6 buys one square of sea, Q26, for 20: Paddy pays 15 and the crown 5. It yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
act("The auctions. Silke puts up her Customs House, minimum 2; Paddy his Wooden Bowl, minimum 6, holding a Bamboo Brush back; Reina her Land Deed Scroll, minimum 6, holding the Vase back. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "The Wooden Bowl goes up this year, starting at six. It's a good bowl, Graham paid nine for its brother once, and I'd rather it went to somebody who'll finish something with it than have it sit on my shelf. The Brush can wait a year. Thank you for the five a square, Graham. That square of rice will pay you back in tax.",
 "Paddy: Two people want the Bowl and only one wants the Brush, so the Bowl's the one that'll fetch a proper price, and I need every grain of it if I'm ever buying back a Jar and a Vase. Six is high enough that I'm not giving it away, and low enough that Silke with twenty-five can still reach it.")
say("Reina", "The Scroll goes up at six. That's what Graham took it to me for once, so it's a fair price with some history to it. The Vase waits a year. It's been sold and resold enough for one table.",
 "Reina: Silke is the only one who needs the Scroll, and every grain she puts into it is a grain she can't bid against me for Paddy's Bowl. I'm keeping the Vase, because two people want it and that's leverage I can use next year.")
say("Silke", "My Customs House goes at two. I don't need it, and I'm not going to bleed anyone for a piece they can't finish a set with. Reina, you and I both want that Bowl. You have the Jar and the Brush and twice my grain, so if you take it, everyone at this table can see how close you are to the throne. Remember, three quarters of every grain we spend bidding against each other goes into the Pile, so I won't chase you past what it's worth to me.",
 "Silke: The Scroll is only any use to me, so I should get it near Reina's six. The Bowl I want badly, but if Reina takes it she becomes the one who looks like she's coming for Graham's throne, and that gives him a better reason to fear her than me. With two new squares of land I'm at plus thirty-five a year, so I'll get my grain back. What I can't buy back is time, and an unfinished canal gives me more of it.")
act("The Storyteller notes: a Wheat set lets Reina replace Silke or Paddy, not the Emperor; at this table only the Silk Peasant may take the throne with a set.", map_hl=[5,7])
say("Paddy", "Two and a half grain doesn't bid on much, so I'm sitting this one out and watching. The Bowl's at six, like I said. Reina, Silke, if you both want it, settle it between you rather than feeding the Pile; it's all the same to me who carries it home, as long as somebody does.",
 "Paddy: If Reina gets the Customs House and my Bowl tonight, she's got the whole wheat set and more than twenty grain, and one of the seats she can take is mine, so I'm selling her the rope, really. But I've nothing to stop her with, and I'd rather have a quarter of six than a bowl gathering dust.")
say("Reina", "Silke, you're right, and I won't pretend otherwise: if I take the Bowl, I'm one claim away from a seat. So here's my offer in public. Stand aside on the Bowl and the Scroll goes at two tonight instead of six, and the Vase goes up at two next year, with nothing from me against you on either. You'd be two pieces closer to the crown for four grain, and the Pile doesn't get fat on our quarrel. If you'd rather fight me for it, I have the grain to win.",
 "Reina: Silke can't go past twenty-five and I can pay twenty-six and still keep twenty-three back for the claim, so the Bowl comes to me either way. Dropping the Scroll to two gives her a reason not to make me pay for it, and it moves her toward Graham's throne instead of my seat.")
lot(5, "CH", [B(7, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Reina; her limit was 4."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on Q17.")), at="Q17")
lot(6, "WB", [
 B(5, 13), B(5, None, "Take it, then. I'll remember what it cost you."),
 B(7, 14)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke stopped at 13; Reina would have gone to 26. The bids were sealed before Reina's offer, so Silke's limit stood."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on Q18.")), at="Q18")
lot(7, "LD", [B(5, 6)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Silke; her limit was 8."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on P22.")), at="P22")
say("Silke", "The Scroll goes on P22, where my Vase used to sit. Reina, I should have taken your deal, and I'll say so in front of everyone. But you've just finished a set that can unseat me or Paddy, and not Graham, so think about who that really helps. If you come for the coast, the crown gets two grateful peasants at your expense. Leave me be, and next year the Vase at two is all I'm asking from you.",
 "Silke: One piece down, three to go, and now the real danger at this table isn't Graham, it's Reina with a full shelf and a choice between my seat and Paddy's. I'm making sure she can see that Graham gains most if she spends her set on me, and Paddy is the softer target if she decides she needs one.")
say("Reina", "Silke, you said yourself that everyone at this table could see how close I was. I'm taking the Silk seat back, the coast I started from. You draw sixty and you get the wheat, the Reservoir I built, four good squares and fifty-five a year, which is better land than I'm getting. Graham, the coast is mine again, and you know what the Silk seat is for. The tax will be what you can show us the numbers for, just as you said.",
 "Reina: The Silk seat is the only door back to the throne, and Silke was standing in it with the Scroll and nineteen grain. Now she's on the wheat with no road up, and I'm holding the Bowl and the Brush, three pieces short of the crown with fifty-six and a half in hand. I've made sure I'm not the one who wasn't counting shelves this time.")
swap(7, 5, "Reina completes the Wheat set (Customs House, Bowl, Brush, Jar) and replaces Silke as the Silk Peasant, in Province 5. She takes Silke's 19.5, leaving her 56.5; the Dragon Jar goes back to Province 5's stock, and her Customs House, Bowl and Brush move to P23, P24 and P25. Silke returns the Land Deed Scroll to stock, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Wheat Peasant, with Province 7.",
 "A complete set and 20 grain in hand let a peasant replace another peasant (Section 11). The displaced player returns everything and draws 60.", at={"CH":"P23", "WB":"P24", "BB":"P25"})
say("Graham", "Reina, six, as I promised. I don't change the price after the work's done. Next year the cards go to Paddy and the bank goes to Silke, three a job, and Paddy keeps the tax. Silke, the Vase and the Scroll are on your shelf now. Set your minimums the way you'd want them set if you were sitting in this chair.",
 "Graham: Reina holds three of the Silk pieces and has fifty-six grain, and the last two she needs are on Silke's shelf. Paying her in full keeps my word, and moving the jobs to Silke and Paddy puts the next year's grain in the hands of the two people who decide what those pieces cost her.")
salaries({5:6, 6:3}, text="Graham pays Reina 6 for the bank and the I Ching, and Paddy 3 for the tax.", hover="Appointees are paid only if the Emperor chooses. The crown ends the year at 17.5.")
act("Graham hands out next year's jobs: Paddy Doyle collects the tax and reads the I Ching; Silke Weber banks the Harvest Pile; 3 a year for each job. Reina holds none.", map_hl=[1,6,7])

# ===== CHAPTER 5: Priced to the Grain
# ============================== YEAR 9 ==============================
R(9, "The rains came late", "dribble1")
act("Paddy turns the eighth I Ching card: Bad Harvest. A bad harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population holds at 300 million, x3. This year's incomes: Graham -30, Reina +10, Paddy +10, Silke +30. The crown holds 17.5 and no realia, so the harvest would leave it at -12.5, in debt. Before the harvest is paid out, the table may give or lend grain.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.\" Population stays at three hundred million, so that's times three, everybody. Read the bad-harvest row at x3. Third rotten year I've turned up for this table, and I'm sorry, I'd have dealt you a better one if the cards let me.",
 "Paddy: Nine grain and another bad year coming in, so I'm counting on the three from each job and Graham's five in salary to keep me out of the Pile. The Vase and the Jar can wait. This year all I'm trying to do is not owe anybody anything.")
act("The Storyteller notes: this is the table's third bad harvest (Years 4, 7 and 9), and Paddy's first reading. An appointed reader's salary is the Emperor's choice; the 'at least 5' is a seated Scholar's due.", map_hl=[1,6])
say("Graham", "No need to apologise, Paddy, you read the card you were dealt. Here's the year: the crown has seventeen and a half and owes thirty, so after the harvest it's twelve and a half in debt. A crown in debt can't pay its share of anyone's squares, and with three hundred million to feed, all of you will need squares. Reina, lend the crown thirteen now and I'll pay you back fifteen out of next year's tax. I paid you exactly what I promised last year, and I'll do it again. Silke, if Reina won't, the same terms go to you.",
 "Graham: If the crown goes into debt, I can't fund a square, and then nobody can buy land in a famine. Thirteen now keeps the chest above zero. I've asked Reina first because she's the one I've paid in full. If Silke turns down a loan that would fund land in her own province, she'll be doing it in front of everyone, same as the canal.")
say("Reina", "Graham, you paid me what you promised and I haven't forgotten it. But every grain I have is already spoken for this year, and I'm not going to pretend otherwise and then come up short at the auction. If Silke wants your terms, they're fair terms. I'm just not the one who can take them this time.",
 "Reina: A crown that's twelve and a half in debt can't outbid anyone for a Vase, and there's only one Vase in China. I'll worry about the debt if I'm the one sitting on it, and right now I'd rather inherit a debt than lose the auction.")
act("The Storyteller notes: the Emperor never bids at auction, whether the crown is in debt or not.", map_hl=[1,5])
say("Silke", "Thirteen it is, Graham, and fifteen back next year. I asked you for thirty once and you said no, so I'm being kinder than you were. One condition, out loud so everyone hears it: a crown that isn't in debt doesn't need ninety percent. I know, because I'm the last one who charged it. Keep the tax decent and we're square.",
 "Silke: Thirteen grain keeps the crown out of the red, and a crown in the red taxes its richest province until it bleeds, which this year is me. It also gets me two grain of interest and puts the Emperor in my debt while I quietly rebuild the Wheat set.")
act("Silke lends the crown 13 before the harvest, on Graham's terms: 15 back from next year's tax.", "Grain may be lent at any time, and no deal is binding (Section 5).", {pid(7): -13, pid(1): 13}, map_hl=[1,7], deal=True)
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it. With Silke's loan the crown covers its -30 and ends at 0.5, out of debt.")
say("Graham", "Thirty percent, Silke, same as last year, nothing like ninety: fifteen grain. Here's the chest: fifteen and a half in, nine out in salaries, three each to Paddy for the tax and the I Ching and to Silke for the bank, and that leaves six and a half. Reina, the canal in your province: thirty-six from you and four from the crown, and it adds ten to your harvest every year from now on. After that the crown pays one a square on your new land. It's not much, but it's what the numbers allow, and the crown stays out of debt so it can pay Silke her fifteen.",
 "Graham: A crown with half a grain can't afford to be generous, so the trick is to be exact. If Reina takes the canal, there's one less work standing between me and the end of the game, and I'll still be on the throne.")
taxes(.30, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Graham's offers for the works step: one section of the Grand Canal in Province 5 (40), Reina 36 and the crown 4, or nothing built this year; and 1 a square from the crown on new land bought by any province this year, for two squares at most in all.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Graham, thank you, and I mean that. I've been asking for that canal since Year 5. But a canal that's thirty-six of mine and four of the crown's isn't the crown's canal, it's my canal with your name on it. Build it when the crown can carry it, and I'll cheer from the coast. Meanwhile I'm keeping my grain on the coast too.",
 "Reina: Thirty-six grain for ten a year in a game that might end next year is a bad trade. Every grain I keep is a grain I can put toward whatever minimum Silke sets on the Vase and the Scroll. And if nothing gets built this year, the game runs one more year, which gives me one more year to get the crown back.")
say("Paddy", "Graham, I've sixteen grain to my name, and the square's twenty. Your one a square won't get me there, so I'm asking four of you, and I'll put in every grain I've got. If the crown can't do four, I'll buy nothing and say no more about it. Times six is still in the deck, and I'd sooner meet it with one more field than with an empty bowl.",
 "Paddy: That's everything I own going into one square of mud next to Q26, but even a bad year brings me ten, and a square pays twenty every year after. The Vase can wait one more year. What I'm afraid of is the times-six card.")
say("Silke", "One square, Graham: nineteen from me and your one. That leaves your other square for Paddy, so the crown can decide whether it stretches to four for him. And don't spend my fifteen doing it.",
 "Silke: One field earns twenty a year and puts me a quarter of the way to surviving a times-six card. The rest of my grain stays dry for the auctions, because the throne gets bought with pieces, not wheat.")
say("Graham", "Paddy, four it is. Here's the count so everyone can check it: fifteen and a half in the chest, nine out in salaries, one for Silke's square and four for yours, and the crown ends the year on one and a half. That's not a grain in debt, Silke, and your fifteen comes out of next year's tax, as agreed. Reina, the canal offer stays open whenever you want it.",
 "Graham: Four grain buys a field in Paddy's province that pays twenty a year, and it shows the table the crown funds land when the numbers allow. Nobody's going to revolt against a man who spent his last spare grain on their fields.")
act("Public works: nothing is built this year; Reina declined to pay 36 of the Province 5 canal section, and the crown wouldn't pay more than 4. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed.", map_hl=[1,5])
buy_land(6, ["Q27"], {6:16, 1:4}, "New land: Province 6 buys one square of sea, Q27, for 20: Paddy pays 16 and the crown 4. It yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
buy_land(7, ["U14"], {7:19, 1:1}, "New land: Province 7 buys one square of the Warlord's land, U14, for 20: Silke pays 19 and the crown 1. It yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
act("The auctions. Each seller puts up one piece: Reina her Dragon Jar and Paddy his Bamboo Brush, the only type each holds; Province 7's Vase was held back last year, and a piece may be held back one round, never two in a row, so Silke puts up the Vase and the Land Deed Scroll waits. Each seller sets their own minimum in their bids. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "My Jar goes up at two, the honest price. Silke, you know what I'm after: the Vase is coming back to the coast where it started, and I'll pay a fair price for it in front of everyone. I'd much rather do that than run you up and feed the Pile.",
 "Reina: I've counted Silke's shelf this time, and the Brush might be the last thing she needs, so if she bids on it she'll be bidding against me. Paddy has nothing and the Emperor can't bid, so the Vase is between me and whatever minimum Silke sets.")
say("Paddy", "I've an empty purse and a new field, so I'm sitting this one out. The Brush is up at two for anybody who wants it, and I'll not be bidding against anyone tonight.",
 "Paddy: All my grain went into Q27, so I can't bid on the Jar or the Vase this year. I'll just watch who takes them, and remember it for when the rice comes in next year.")
say("Silke", "Paddy's chest is empty and Graham's minding the crown's, so I'll say it plainly: I'm bidding on the Jar and the Brush, and I'm not getting into a war over either. The Vase goes up at two. Whoever wants it can have it cheap.",
 "Silke: With Paddy at zero and Graham guarding ten grain, the only person who can push my prices up is Reina, and she'd be paying three quarters of it into the pile just to spite me. Two pieces for a few grain each would put me halfway back to the coast.")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(7, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Silke; her limit was 20."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on R15.")), at="R15")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 15), B(7, None, "That's more than it's worth to me. Enjoy it."),
 B(5, 16)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke stopped at 15; Reina bid only to keep it from Silke, up to 25. Reina already holds a Brush."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on P26.")), at="P26")
lot(7, "VA", [B(5, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody bid against Reina; her limit was 42."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on P22.")), at="P22")
say("Silke", "Sixteen grain for a brush you'll never use, Reina, just so I can't have it. Twelve of that went into the pile. Keep spending like that and you'll win the argument and lose the coast. Thank you for the Jar, though.",
 "Silke: The Jar cost me two and Reina paid sixteen to block the Brush, so she's the one bleeding now. I've also learned she's guarding the coast against me, so from here on I keep my bids quiet.")
say("Reina", "Silke, two for the Vase, and that's generous of you, I won't forget it. The Brush I paid for in fear, sixteen grain against a shelf I counted wrong. Fair enough, Paddy, it went into your pocket and not into the river. That leaves one Scroll and twenty grain, and everyone here knows where the Scroll is.",
 "Reina: Silke was four pieces away and I spent sixteen grain blocking a door that was never open. That's the price of finally paying attention, and I'll take it, because I've got fifty-two grain and the Vase is home, and all that's left is the Scroll on Silke's shelf next year.")
act("The Storyteller notes: a seller keeps a quarter of the price; of Reina's 16 for the Brush, Paddy keeps 4 and 12 goes to the Harvest Pile.", map_hl=[5,6])
salaries({6:6, 7:3}, text="Graham pays Paddy 6 for the tax and the I Ching, and Silke 3 for the bank, as he said.", hover="Appointees are paid only if the Emperor chooses. The crown ends the year at 1.5, and owes Silke 15 from next year's tax.")

# ============================== YEAR 10 ==============================
R(10, "Heaven has favourites", "heaven")
act("Paddy turns the ninth I Ching card: Heaven Has Favourites. A good harvest for Province 5 and a bad one for Provinces 1, 6 and 7; nobody plays a disaster. The population holds at 300 million, x3. This year's incomes: Graham -30, Reina +60, Paddy +30, Silke +50. The crown holds 1.5 and no realia, so the harvest would leave it at -28.5, in debt. Before the harvest is paid out, the table may give or lend grain.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "\"Heaven Has Favourites. Some fields shine, some fields sulk.\" Provinces 2, 4 and 5 get a good harvest. Provinces 1, 3, 6, 7 and 8 get a bad one. Population stays at three hundred million, times three, so read the x3 row. Well, Reina, the sun's on the silk this year and the rain's on the rest of us. Graham, that's your north under the Wall gone sulky too, so I'd look to your bowl before you look to anybody's square.",
 "Paddy: That's my own paddies on the bad row, with ten grain to my name and no Jar or Vase in sight. It's Reina with the good harvest and fifty-two grain, one Scroll short of the set, so she's the one I'm watching.")
act("The Storyteller notes: an appointed reader's salary is the Emperor's choice; the 'at least 5' is a seated Scholar's due.", map_hl=[1,6])
say("Graham", "The crown holds one and a half, and the north is down thirty, so I'll ask for exactly what it needs: twenty-nine before the harvest, thirty-three back out of next year's tax, after Silke's fifteen. Reina, you're the one province Heaven smiled on, so I'm asking you first, and in front of everyone. A crown in debt can't put a grain into anyone's field at the next population card, and that includes yours.",
 "Graham: This year's tax on a hundred and forty of income is about forty-two, which covers Silke's fifteen and leaves enough to start on Reina's thirty-three, so the numbers hold. If Reina won't lend to the crown, she's telling us she means to take it, and the whole table will have heard her say no.")
say("Reina", "Twenty-nine it is, Graham, and on your terms: thirty-three back after Silke's fifteen. I said in Year 7 that a crown in debt is bad for every field in China, and I'm not going to argue the other side of it now because the sun happens to be on my silk. Everyone heard the terms, so everyone can count them.",
 "Reina: Whoever sits on that throne at the next population card has to feed Province 1 first, and I mean for it to be me, so I'd rather it came to me solvent with thirty-three owed to my name than buried under twenty-eight of debt. After the harvest I'll still have eighty-three, which covers any minimum Silke puts on that Scroll plus the twenty to claim, and leaves some over.")
act("Reina lends the crown 29 before the harvest, on Graham's terms: 33 back from the crown's tax, after Silke's 15.", "Grain may be lent at any time, and no deal is binding (Section 5).", {pid(5): -29, pid(1): 29}, map_hl=[1,5], deal=True)
say("Silke", "Well lent, Reina. That's the crown out of the red, with my fifteen first in line, everyone heard it. I'll keep my grain in the sack this year. Somebody at this table has to have some left when the population card comes round.",
 "Silke: Reina just paid twenty-nine grain to look like the table's conscience, and she's one Scroll short of a set. That Scroll sits on my shelf, so every grain she spends being generous is a grain she won't have when it comes up for auction.")
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it. With Reina's loan the crown covers its -30 and ends at 0.5, out of debt.")
say("Graham", "Forty percent, and here's every grain of it. Fifty-six comes in. Fifteen goes to Silke and thirty-three to Reina, both in full, today, which leaves eight and a half. Nothing gets built this year because I won't start a forty-grain work on money I've already promised away, and I'll pay one a square on anyone's new land.",
 "Graham: Thirty percent wouldn't have covered both loans, and I've never once paid a debt late at this table, so I'm not going to start the year the crown owes the two richest players in China. Province 1 is still five short and I can't buy its square this year, so next year's tax has to buy the north its square.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Graham repays both loans from the tax, in full: 15 to Silke and 33 to Reina. The crown holds 8.5.", "No deal is binding (Section 5), but this one is kept.", {pid(1): -48, pid(7): 15, pid(5): 33}, map_hl=[1,5,7], deal=True)
act("Graham's offers for the works step: nothing is built this year; the crown pays 1 a square on new land bought by any other province, and buys none for Province 1.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "One square of sea at N28, Graham, nineteen from me and your one from the crown, as you offered. I'm not going to stand here with ninety grain and let the coast go hungry, but I'm not going to pave it either, because there's a Scroll still to come this year.",
 "Reina: One square puts grain to work and keeps the coast ahead of the next population card, and I still have seventy-three, so Silke would have to ask more than fifty for that Scroll before I couldn't pay it and still have the twenty to claim. If she sets it that high, everyone at the table will see she's pricing a crown and not a piece of paper.")
buy_land(5, ["N28"], {5:19, 1:1}, "New land: Province 5 buys one square of sea, N28, for 20: Reina pays 19 and the crown 1. It yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
say("Paddy", "Same as Reina, Graham: one square of sea at R27, nineteen from me and your one from the crown. I've twenty-eight grain, and I'd sooner it went into a field that feeds the south-east than sat in a sack while the two of you bid over a Scroll I can't use.",
 "Paddy: I can't reach the set from here with the Jar and the Vase both on other shelves, so the best I can do with the grain is turn it into rice. One more square means if that population card ever doubles us again, the paddies won't eat me hollow the way they nearly did in Year 6.")
buy_land(6, ["R27"], {6:19, 1:1}, "New land: Province 6 buys one square of sea, R27, for 20: Paddy pays 19 and the crown 1. It yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
say("Silke", "Two squares for the wheat, Graham, U15 and U16, nineteen each from me and your one a square, as you offered. The rain sulked on Province 7 this year, so I'm digging deeper before the next population card arrives, not after.",
 "Silke: Thirty-eight grain into the ground still leaves me fifty-seven and a half, more than enough to make Reina pay full price for that Scroll. And it's two squares closer to surviving a doubling that would sink everyone else.")
buy_land(7, ["U15", "U16"], {7:38, 1:2}, "New land: Province 7 buys two squares of the Warlord's land, U15 and U16, for 40: Silke pays 38 and the crown 2. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
act("Public works: nothing is built this year. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed. Land: 22 squares.", map_hl=[1])
act("The auctions. Provinces 5 and 6 have no stock left, and the Harvest Pile holds none. Province 7's Land Deed Scroll was held back last year, and a piece may be held back one round, never two in a row, so it goes up this year; Silke sets its minimum in her bids. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Everyone knows what this one is for, so I won't pretend otherwise. Silke, set your price where you like. I've kept my twenty for the claim, and whatever's left over that is yours to name.",
 "Reina: Fifty-three is everything I have once the twenty for the claim is set aside, and Paddy's nine can't get anywhere near it, so the only person who can stop me now is Silke, with her own minimum. If she prices it out of my reach, she's giving up a quarter of a sale and keeping a piece she can't use, all to keep Graham on the throne. Good luck explaining that to the table.")
say("Paddy", "I've nine grain and a Scroll already on my paddies, so I'll not push the price up on a piece I can't use. Bidding just to feed the Pile isn't my way. Reina, if it's yours, you'll have got it fair, and I'll say so.",
 "Paddy: Reina's had her twenty set aside for weeks, and with my nine I couldn't stop her even if I tried, so the honest thing is to sit still and keep my grain. Once she's climbed, the Scroll and the Customs House on my paddies are still worth something to whoever's chasing a set next.")
say("Silke", "You told me to name my price, Reina, so here it is: fifty-three. That leaves you exactly the twenty for your claim and not a grain over. You said in Year 8 you'd never forget the Vase at two. This is me remembering it for you.",
 "Silke: Seventy-three less twenty is fifty-three, so that's the most she can pay and still climb. If she pays it she reaches the upper class with an empty sack and I get thirteen back, and if she won't, she waits a whole year while I grow.")
lot(7, "LD", [B(5, 53)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's minimum was 53; Reina's sealed limit was 53, so she takes it at the minimum. Silke keeps a quarter, 13; 40 goes to the Harvest Pile."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on P21.")), at="P21")
say("Reina", "Fifty-three, Silke, and that's fair: you priced it to the grain, and I'd have done exactly the same. Graham, the Silk seat is yours again, and so is the coast I just put a new square on, so you won't be taking over an empty sack. The crown's coming back to where it started, and the first thing it does is pay its appointees and look north.",
 "Reina: Three years off the throne, and I got back by counting every shelf at the table, mine included. I take over a thin crown with four and a half in it, a Wall and a Grand Canal still owed, and the hungriest province in China, so the hard part starts now.")
swap(5, 1, "Reina completes the Silk set (Bowl, Brush, Vase, Scroll) with 20 in hand and takes the throne back. Every piece she held goes back to stock. She takes the crown's reserve as it stands, 4.5, leaving her 24.5. Graham draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Silk Peasant, with Province 5.",
 "At a table with no upper class the Silk Peasant may take the throne with a complete set and 20 grain in hand. Becoming Emperor returns every piece; the old Emperor hands over the reserve and draws 60.")
say("Reina", "The crown pays what the crown promised, even when the promise was Graham's: six to Paddy and three to Silke. Same hands on the same jobs next year at the same three a job, because nothing worked last year that needs fixing. Paddy, I've known a crown that would give you nothing for reading a card. I wasn't that crown before and I'm not going to be now.",
 "Reina: That leaves fifteen and a half in the reserve against a north that loses five in an average year, so I'm not buying land I'd have to go into debt to pay for. Paddy's the poorest at the table, so the salaried jobs go to him, because a peasant with nine grain and nobody paying him is how a revolt starts.")
salaries({6:6, 7:3}, text="Reina pays Paddy 6 for the tax and the I Ching, and Silke 3 for the bank, as Graham had paid them.", hover="Appointees are paid only if the Emperor chooses. The crown ends the year at 15.5.")
act("Reina keeps the table jobs where they are for next year: Paddy Doyle collects the tax and reads the I Ching; Silke Weber banks the Harvest Pile; 3 a year for each job. Graham holds none.", map_hl=[1,6,7])

# ===== CHAPTER 6: Two for the Bowl
act("The table gathers before Year 11's card is drawn. Before Paddy turns it, the four may agree to stop the game where it sits and score it (Note 19, Section 18); every player must agree, or play goes on into Year 11.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "I'll agree to stop here, and I'm not going to pretend it's out of generosity: I sat down tonight on the throne and I'm sitting on it now. Silke, you gave me the hardest fifty-three grain I've ever paid, and Graham, that loan was the cleanest deal of the night. If anyone wants an eleventh year, say so, and I'll rule it as well as I can.",
 "Reina: Stopping now hands me the win, so of course I'm saying yes. The honest thing is to say that in front of everyone and let each of them decide whether one more card is worth the risk for them.")
say("Graham", "No. I sat down tonight as the Emperor and I'm standing up as a man with sixty grain and a coast, and that isn't a finish I'll sign. One more year, Reina. Paddy, turn the card.",
 "Graham: The scoring is plain: I drew the throne, so the only result that counts for me is holding it, and stopping here locks in my loss. Every piece just went back to stock, I have the most grain of any peasant, and the one year it takes to rebuild a set is the year I'm asking for.")
say("Paddy", "I'd stop here. I sat down as the Rice Peasant and I'm still him, with more paddy than I started with and nobody's grain in my pocket but my own, and I'm content with that. But it takes all four of us, Graham, and you've said no, so if it's another year, I'll turn the card and read it as straight as I read the last two.",
 "Paddy: Stopping now means I hold my province, and with fifteen grain and the Jar and the Vase on Silke's shelves, a hold is about as good as I'm going to do this year. Graham's no means it's going on anyway, so I'll put my hand on the deck and hope the rice gets a good card for once.")
say("Silke", "I'm with Graham. I've had a throne at this table and tonight I'm standing in a wheat field, and I won't sign for that either. Seventy-three grain says I'm not finished, Reina. Turn the card, Paddy.",
 "Silke: Graham's no already keeps the game going, but I want it on the record that I said no too: this is the richest I've been all night, and Province 7 is no seat to finish in. One more year with the Bowl and the Brushes back in Province 6's stock is a year I can use.")
act("Reina and Paddy agree to stop; Graham and Silke decline. The table is asked once more.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Then that's fair, Graham, Silke. Neither of you owes me a signature, and I wouldn't have signed in your seats either. Turn the card, Paddy. The crown taxes what Heaven gives it, pays its appointees, and looks north, the same as it did in Year 1.",
 "Reina: The two richest people at the table both want my chair, and the crown has fifteen and a half in it, so Year 11 comes down to whether I can make the other two afraid of each other before either of them gets around to me. I've played that game from this throne before, and this time I'm watching both of their shelves from the first card.")
act("The Storyteller notes to Graham: he drew the Wheat Peasant (Province 7), not the throne, so the Silk seat (5) he holds now would count as a win if the game stopped here (Section 18); and Silke holds 73.5 grain to his 60.", map_hl=[5,7])
say("Graham", "I stand corrected, and I'll say it in front of everyone: I sat down in the wheat, not on the throne, and I counted from the wrong chair. On those numbers I'll sign. Silke, it's your word now, and I won't pretend I'm asking for your sake.",
 "Graham: The Storyteller's right, I drew Province 7, and the Silk seat at 5 is a step up from where I started, so stopping here is a win for me. Arguing over a year I've already won would be the worst count I've made all night.")
say("Paddy", "I'll say the same as before: I'd stop here, and I'm glad of where I'm standing. Silke, it's your word now, and I'll not try to talk you round. If it's no, I've the deck in my hand and I'll turn it fair.",
 "Paddy: Graham's changed his mind, so it's three of us for stopping now, and if Silke says yes I finish where I started, as the Rice Peasant with no debts. If she wants one more year to get her throne back, I can't blame her, because I've wanted a set myself all night.")
say("Silke", "Of course you'll sign now, Graham, you started in my wheat and you're finishing on my coast. I sat down tonight as the Silk Peasant and I'm not standing up two provinces further south. One more year, everyone. Paddy, turn the card.",
 "Silke: Three of them stop ahead of where they sat down, and I'm the only one who'd stop behind, so I'm the only one with a reason to keep playing. With seventy-three grain, the best farm on the board, and the Bowl and Brushes back in stock, one more year is the best chance I've had since Year 3.")
act("Reina, Graham and Paddy agree to stop; Silke declines. Every player must agree, so the game doesn't stop here: play goes on into Year 11.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])

# ============================== YEAR 11 ==============================
R(11, "The population doubles", "double3")
act("Paddy turns the tenth I Ching card: Population Doubles. A good harvest for every province, and no disasters. The population doubles to 600 million, x6, and every province eats twice what it ate last year. This year's incomes: Reina -160, Graham -40, Paddy -20, Silke +20. Before the harvest is paid out, the table may give or lend grain.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "\"Population Doubles. Fat years make large families. All provinces get a good harvest, and every province's consumption doubles with it.\" So the counter goes from times three to times six. That's six hundred million, everyone, so read the times-six row on your tables. It's a good harvest, at least, though I'll tell you now there are a lot more mouths at my table than there were an hour ago.",
 "Paddy: It's a good harvest, so the rice comes in fat this year, but from now on twice as many people eat it, and my fifteen grain needs to go into paddy before next year's bowls come round. Silke wanted one more year, and she's handed every one of us a much hungrier one.")
say("Reina", "I'll say the number before anyone else has to. The north eats a hundred and sixty this year, the crown holds fifteen and a half, and nobody at this table has a hundred and forty-five to spare without going hungry themselves. So I'm not asking. Graham, I lent to you last year because a crown in debt is an empty prize, and I won't make you pay that back by emptying your own table. Pay it out, Paddy. The crown takes what's coming to it, and we'll talk tax and land once the grain's on the table.",
 "Reina: That's the revolt clause I promised myself I'd see coming, and here it is. Any loan I could get tonight is a drop against a hundred and sixty, and it would only drag Graham or Paddy into debt with me, so I'm keeping everyone else fed and saving my words for the tax step, where Silke's harvest is the only one that can pay for anything.")
say("Graham", "Thank you for saying it plainly, Reina, and for not asking. My coast eats forty this year, which leaves me twenty. That's one square of sea and nothing over, so I've nothing to lend tonight, and I won't pretend I have. Pay it out, Paddy.",
 "Graham: Sixty less forty is twenty, and twenty is exactly one square, so every grain I lend is land I don't buy. I'm watching what the crown can offer per square, because a throne a hundred and forty-five in debt can't pay anyone's share.")
say("Paddy", "I'll say my number plainly too. The south-east eats twenty this year and I've fifteen in the sack, so I'm five short, and five short means my Scroll and my Customs House go back to the Pile for two apiece. Silke, you're the only one at this table coming out ahead this year. Lend me five and I'll pay you six out of next year's rice. You all know I've never yet left a debt standing.",
 "Paddy: Five grain is all that stands between me and the Pile taking the two pieces I've been holding since Year 1, and Silke's the only one with grain to spare, which means she's also the one sitting on the Jar and the Vase I need. Better I owe her a fair six than lose the set for four.")
say("Silke", "Paddy, I won't lend you five. I'll buy you out of trouble. Six grain for the Customs House on R24, right now. That's three times what the Pile would give you, and your Scroll stays on the board. If you'd rather keep the house, fine, I'll lend you the five for six next year like you said. But the six for the house is the better deal for both of us, and you know it.",
 "Silke: If he's short, that Customs House goes back to the Pile for two, and then I'm bidding against everyone for it at auction. Six now gets me one of my three missing pieces without a fight, and the Bowl and Brushes are sitting in his stock, so they come up when Province 6 sells.")
say("Paddy", "That's a fair offer, Silke, and I thank you for making it in the open. But the house stays on R24. I'll take the five on the terms I named, six back out of next year's rice, and the whole table can hold me to it.",
 "Paddy: Six grain for the Customs House would have handed Silke a piece she needs, and she's already holding the Jar, the Vase and a Scroll, so I'd be passing her the set across the table. I hate owing anybody, but I'd sooner owe her six grain than give her the house.")
act("Silke lends Paddy 5 before the harvest, on his terms: 6 back from next year's harvest. Both of Paddy's pieces stay on the board.", "Grain may be lent at any time, and no deal is binding (Section 5).", {pid(7): -5, pid(6): 5}, map_hl=[6,7], deal=True)
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it. The crown can't cover its -160 from the 15.5 it holds, and has no realia on the board to sell back, so it ends at -144.5, in debt. Graham ends at 20, Paddy at 0 and Silke at 88.5.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent, and only Silke owes it, which is eight grain against a hundred and forty-four. I'm not going to pretend that clears anything, and I won't squeeze the one table that's eating well to make a gesture. Nothing gets built and nobody's land gets funded this year. That isn't me refusing you. The rules shut the crown's purse the moment it went under, and I'd fund a share of every square at this table if I had a grain to do it with. Paddy, Silke, your three a job stands. If the crown can't hand it over tonight, it's written down as owed, and you've seen me pay a debt before.",
 "Reina: A ninety percent tax would bring in eighteen. That's nowhere near enough to save me, and it would hand Silke a grievance to go with her eighty-eight grain, so I'm taking a fair rate and keeping my reputation, which is the only thing I've got left. The real danger now is a revolt over the land I can't fund, so I've put it on the record that the rules stopped me and I didn't.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("The tax goes against the crown's debt: it now holds -136.5. Public works: nothing is built this year, since a crown in debt can't pay a share of any work. New land: none this year for any province, since a crown in debt can't pay its share of any square. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed. Land: 22 squares.", "A player in debt can't fund works, and every province's square needs some share from the crown (Section 13, Note 7).", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "One Bamboo Brush from the south-east, starting at four. I've a debt to pay Silke next year and I mean to pay it, so I'm glad of any honest bid, and I won't be bidding on my own lot with an empty sack.",
 "Paddy: Silke's set needs a Customs House, a Brush and my Bowl, and she's the only one at the table with grain, so a Brush brings in something towards what I owe her. The Bowl stays on my shelf, and whoever wants that last piece will have to come and talk to me about it.")
say("Silke", "The Vase goes up at twenty-one, and I won't take a grain less. I sold one at two once and I've been hearing about it ever since. And Reina, I'd have bought my own square tonight with all twenty from my own sack, but the rules won't let me while the crown's under. So that's one more reason for all of us to want that chest back above zero.",
 "Silke: Graham sits in the Silk seat with twenty grain, so a Vase at twenty-one stays on my shelf and his set doesn't get any closer. I can't buy land until Reina's crown is out of debt, so the eighty I'm holding has to last me through whatever the next card brings.")
say("Graham", "My Customs House goes up at two. It's my only piece, and I'd rather it went to a table that'll use it than sat on my shelf. Paddy, I'll bid honestly on your brush, and I'll bid within what I've got. Silke, twenty-one for a Vase is a fair price for someone with eighty in the sack. I have twenty, and that's one square of sea the day the crown can pay its share.",
 "Graham: A brush for a few grain is the cheapest step toward a set I'll see this year, and I've watched Silke buy a brush once just to keep it from me, so she won't do it free a second time. The Vase I leave alone: twenty-one is every grain I own, and grain is what buys land when the chest comes back above zero.")
say("Paddy", "I've an empty sack tonight, so I'm only selling. The Brush stands at four, like I said. Silke, that Vase is the piece I've wanted since Year 3, and it hurts to sit on my hands while it goes round, but I don't bid grain I haven't got.",
 "Paddy: With nothing in the sack, the best I can do is let the Brush earn a bit towards the six I owe Silke and watch who goes for the Vase. If Graham ends up with it, at least it's with a man who once stood aside for me.")
say("Silke", "I'll be honest about this one, since I promised myself I wouldn't announce anything and I'm going to anyway: I want that Brush, Graham. You've got twenty and I've got more than twenty. Let me have it cheap and I'll remember it when your coast needs a lender.",
 "Silke: Nobody else can bid on Graham's Customs House, so it should come to me at the minimum, and with twenty-one on the Brush I'm one grain past everything Graham has. After that the only thing between me and a set is Paddy's Bowl and twenty grain.")
lot(5, "CH", [B(7, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham's minimum was 2, and only Silke bid: Graham can't bid on his own lot and Paddy holds no grain. Graham keeps a quarter, 0.5; 1.5 goes to the Harvest Pile."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on R16.")), at="R16")
lot(6, "BB", [B(5, 4), B(7, 5), B(5, 8), B(7, 9),
 B(5, None, "That's past what it's worth to me. Take it.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 4. Graham's limit was 6, or 8 to keep it from Silke; Silke's was 21, so she takes it at one step over Graham's 8. Paddy keeps a quarter, 2; 7 goes to the Harvest Pile."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on R14.")), at="R14")
say("Silke", "Two for the house and nine for the brush. Thank you, Graham, and thank you, Paddy. I'll put them next to the Jar where everyone can see them. I'm not hiding anything this time.",
 "Silke: That's the Jar, the Customs House and the Brush in a row, and it cost me eleven. All I need now is Paddy's Bowl and twenty grain, and with sixty-nine and a half in the sack I can afford to pay whatever it takes when that Bowl comes up.")
act("Nobody bids on Silke's Vase at her minimum of 21, so it stays in Province 7's stock and must go up again next year at 2.", map_hl=[7])
act("Salaries: the crown is in debt, so as Reina said, the jobs' pay goes on the slate: the crown owes Paddy 6 (the tax and the I Ching) and Silke 3 (the bank), 3 a job, to be paid when it can. The jobs stay as they are for Year 12: Paddy collects the tax and reads the I Ching; Silke banks the Harvest Pile.", "An appointee's pay is the Emperor's choice (Note 3); a crown in debt pays out nothing.", map_hl=[1,6,7])

act("The table gathers again before Year 12's card is drawn. Before Paddy turns it, the four may agree to stop the game where it sits and score it (Note 19, Section 18); every player must agree, or play goes on into Year 12.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "I'll agree again, and I'll be as plain about it as I was the last two times: stopping now hands me the win, so of course I'll sign. But look at the shelves before you answer. Silke has a Jar, a Customs House and a Brush on the board, eighty-odd in the sack, and the only crown at this table is a hundred and thirty-six in debt. If we play on, the crown can't build, can't fund a single square and can't pay its own people. Graham, Paddy, you've already said yes. Silke, I know what it'll be, but it's your word.",
 "Reina: I counted Silke's shelf this time, out loud, so nobody can say later that I didn't see her coming. Three pieces up and a full sack against a bankrupt throne. If she says no, I'm playing Year 12 for my reputation and nothing else.")
say("Graham", "I'll sign, same as last time. Silke, that's the second brush you've taken from me by one grain, and I've counted both. Reina's right about the shelves, though. Another year means a crown that can't pay, a coast that eats sixty-five, and you with three pieces up. I can see what that adds up to, and so can you.",
 "Graham: I started in Province 7 and I'm sitting in Province 5, so stopping now is a win for me, and Silke's Brush, Jar and Customs House only matter if the game keeps going. Every year we play on, I have twenty and a half grain and she has seventy, and I don't need to work out who that favours.")
say("Paddy", "I'll sign, same as the last two times. Silke, I owe you six from next year's rice, and if we stop here there's no next year to pay it from, so you'll have the two in my sack tonight and my word on the other four, which I mean to keep. I'm still the Rice Peasant and I've still got my paddies, and I'm content with that.",
 "Paddy: Another year at times six would eat forty-five out of a sack holding two, and the Pile would have my Scroll and my House before the card was cold, so stopping here is the only way the south-east comes through whole. What sits badly with me is the debt to Silke, so I'll pay her what I can tonight.")
say("Silke", "No. Not with three pieces on the board and one to go. Reina, you'd decline too if you were sitting in my chair, and you know it. Paddy, keep your two, you'll need them more than I will. One more year, everyone, and then I'll be the one asking you to stop.",
 "Silke: All I need is Paddy's Bowl and twenty grain, and I've got nearly seventy, so there's nobody at this table who can outbid me. The only risk is the card, and wheat that earns minus five in an ordinary year can stand a lot more than an empty crown and a hungry coast can.")
act("Reina, Graham and Paddy agree to stop; Silke declines. The table is asked once more.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Still yes, and Silke, you're right: in your chair I'd say no too, and I wouldn't apologise for it. So let's not pretend there's a deal to be had here. You've earned the right to turn one more card. Paddy, when she's ready, turn it fair, and the crown will take whatever Heaven sends it, from a hundred and thirty-six under.",
 "Reina: There's nothing I can offer Silke that's worth more than a throne one piece away, and an offer she'd laugh at would only make the crown look desperate. Better to concede it gracefully, keep the table's respect, and see which piece she still needs and who's holding it.")
say("Graham", "Yes, still. I won't try to buy your word, Silke. I've got twenty and a half, and you'd laugh at it. But you said it yourself: one piece to go. I'll be watching which one, and what you pay for it.",
 "Graham: Every time we stop, I'm winning, so I'll go on signing however often they ask. If she plays on, her last piece comes out of Paddy's stock or the Pile's, and an empty sack can still bid it up a grain or two.")
say("Paddy", "Still yes. Silke, I'll say this to everyone so nobody can say I said it behind their back: the piece you're missing is my Wooden Bowl, and it goes up at the auction like anything else, at a price I'll name when I get there. If it's no, I've got the deck in my hand and I'll turn it fair.",
 "Paddy: Silke's last piece is on my shelf, and the rules say it can only leave there at auction, so for once the Rice Peasant has something the whole table will be watching. I'll keep my two grain against the debt, and I'll make whoever wants that Bowl pay what it's worth.")
say("Silke", "Still no. Turn it, Paddy. And since you've said it in front of everyone, I'll say this in front of everyone too: put the Bowl up next year rather than the Brush, and the six you owe me is forgiven, on top of whatever I pay for it. Name your price. I'll give you a fair one.",
 "Silke: Paddy has two pieces in stock and he chooses which one goes up, so the only way he stops me is by holding the Bowl back a year. Forgiving his six is cheap if it gets that Bowl onto the table.")
act("Reina, Graham and Paddy agree to stop; Silke declines again. Every player must agree, so the game doesn't stop here: play would go on into Year 12.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])

# ============================== YEAR 12 ==============================
R(12, "Fortune and misfortune", "goodevil")
act("Paddy turns the eleventh I Ching card: Fortune and Misfortune. A good harvest for every province; Provinces 2, 4, 6 and 8 play a personal disaster, and of those only Province 6 is seated, so Paddy plays the first disaster in the Rice Peasant's booklet, 4, after the harvest. The population holds at 600 million, x6. This year's incomes: Reina -160, Graham -40, Paddy -20, Silke +20. Before the harvest is paid out, the table may give or lend grain.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "Fortune and Misfortune. Plenty for everyone, and trouble for some. All provinces collect a good harvest, so read the good-harvest row. Population stays at six hundred million, that's times six. And provinces 2, 4, 6 and 8 play a Personal Disaster. Two and four are empty, eight isn't at this table... so that's six. That's me. Well, I said I'd turn it fair. Silke, I heard you. The Bowl goes up this year whatever happens, and we'll talk price when we get there.",
 "Paddy: Good harvest for the whole of China, and I'm the only one at the table the trouble lands on. It's my own hand that turned it, so I can hardly complain. I'll read my disaster out straight, same as the card, and hope the good rice covers whatever it costs me.")
say("Reina", "Nobody lends into a hole a hundred and sixty deep, and I won't ask anyone to. The north takes its loss and the crown keeps its books honest. Silke, though, Paddy is the one at the edge this year: two grain, twenty owed, and a disaster still to read. If you want that Bowl at a fair price, a fair hand to him now would be the cheapest grain you spend all year.",
 "Reina: The crown can't be saved this year, so I'm doing what an Empress still can do, which is keep the rest of the table fed and stop Paddy selling his shelf back to the pile for two a piece. If Silke lends to him she's paying for her own Bowl in public, and if she won't, everyone sees that she let him go under.")
say("Graham", "Forty out, twenty and a half in. I'll be nineteen and a half under, and I've no piece on the board to sell. I could ask Silke for twenty, but I can't show anyone a harvest of mine that pays it back next year, so I won't ask. I'll carry it myself, in the open, where you can all count it.",
 "Graham: A loan I can't repay is just a debt with someone else's name on it, and I've kept every promise at this table by only making the ones I could count. Being in debt keeps me out of the auction this year, which matters less than you'd think, because with twenty grain I was never going to win one.")
say("Paddy", "I'll not pretend otherwise: two grain, twenty out, and a disaster still to read. Without help the Pile takes my Deed and my Customs House at two apiece. Silke, would you lend me eighteen now? You'll have it back at the auction, out of whatever the Bowl brings and the six the crown owes me, and the six I already owe you as well. I'll not ask you to forgive anything. I'd sooner pay what I owe.",
 "Paddy: Silke wants the Bowl, and I'm the man who has it, so this is the one year she's likely to lend into a hole. I'd rather owe her honestly for a fortnight than watch the paddies give up the Deed and the House for four grain.")
say("Silke", "Eighteen, Paddy, and you're right not to ask me to forgive it. Twenty-four comes back to me at the auction, and when you price that Bowl, remember who kept your Deed off the Pile. Reina, thank you for the advice. I'd have done it without you, but it's nice to see the crown being generous with my grain.",
 "Silke: Look at the table: Reina and Graham are both going under this harvest, and a player in debt can't bid, so once the grain's paid out I'm the only buyer that Bowl has. Eighteen keeps Paddy on his feet and keeps him friendly, and the price he sets on the Bowl is now the last thing between me and the climb.")
act("Silke lends Paddy 18 before the harvest, to be repaid at this year's auction with the 6 he already owes her, 24 in all.", "Grain may be lent at any time, and no deal is binding (Section 5).", {pid(7): -18, pid(6): 18}, map_hl=[6,7], deal=True)
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it. The crown can't cover its -160 and has no realia to sell back, so it ends at -296.5, in debt. Graham can't cover his -40 and holds no realia, so he ends at -19.5, in debt. Paddy's -20 takes him to exactly 0, and Silke ends at 71.5.")
sell_back(pid(6), ["LD", "CH"], "Paddy's disaster is still to be paid, 4, and he holds 0: one piece back at 2 wouldn't cover it, so both go back to the Harvest Pile at 2 each, the Land Deed Scroll from R23 and the Customs House from R24.", "A payment that would take a player below zero is covered first by selling realia on the board back to the pile at 2 each, until it's covered or nothing is left.")
disaster(6, "the only water buffalo breaks its leg in the middle of planting.", "The first disaster in the Rice Peasant's booklet. Paddy ends at 0, out of debt, with no realia on the board.")
say("Reina", "Ten percent, the lowest the law allows. Silke, that's two grain out of your twenty, and it goes straight into a hole three hundred deep, so I won't take a grain more than I have to. No works and no land this year, and that's not the crown refusing anyone. The rules shut my purse the moment the north went under, and I'd rather say it to your faces than pretend otherwise. Paddy, Silke, your pay is written down and still owed. The crown doesn't forget its debts just because it can't pay them yet.",
 "Reina: A crown at minus two hundred and ninety-six can't buy loyalty, so the cheapest thing I can do is take as little as possible and give nobody a grievance before the vote. All I can do now is keep the table calm and hope they vote to stop before the Bowl decides who comes after me.")
taxes(.10, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("The tax goes against the crown's debt: it now holds -294.5. Public works: nothing is built this year, since a crown in debt can't pay a share of any work. New land: none this year for any province, since a crown in debt can't pay its share of any square. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed. Land: 22 squares.", "A player in debt can't fund works, and every province's square needs some share from the crown.", map_hl=[1])
say("Graham", "I'm nineteen and a half under, so I can't bid, and you can all see that. Silke, the Bowl's yours to lose. Just remember a set wins nothing without a seat that's worth taking, and the throne is three hundred grain under.",
 "Graham: The law has me sitting out, so the best I can do is point at the hole the throne's in before anyone pays for a set to climb into it. Silke's lending to Paddy and courting him over the Bowl, and I'm counting what it costs her.")
say("Paddy", "The Bowl goes up at two, Silke, and that's not me being careless with it. Three parts of whatever you pay goes into the Pile and only one to me, so a high price would only cost you four grain for every one I'd get. You kept my Deed off the Pile, or tried to, and I'll not make you pay twice over for it. Every grain the Bowl brings me goes straight back to you, and so does the six the crown owes me, the day it can pay. I'm passing on the rest. I've nothing to bid with, and I'll not bid with grain I owe.",
 "Paddy: The buffalo took the Deed and the House anyway, so the set's gone, and all that's left is to pay my debts honestly. Two is the fairest price I can give the woman who lent into my hole, and I'll stay the Rice Peasant who pays back what he owes.")
say("Silke", "Two it is, Paddy, and thank you. That's the first fair price anyone's offered me in this game. Graham, I heard you. A seat three hundred under is still a seat, and I'd rather owe the empire's debts than watch somebody else sit on them.",
 "Silke: Everyone else at this table is in debt or holding nothing, so the Bowl comes to me at whatever Paddy asks, and the ceiling of ten is only there in case something surprises me. That gives me the Jar, the Customs House, the Brush and the Bowl with nearly seventy grain behind them, and the Pile can keep its leftovers: I'm not buying a single piece I don't need.")
act("The Storyteller notes: a completed Wheat set lets Silke replace the Silk Peasant or the Rice Peasant (Graham or Paddy), never the Emperor; at this table only the Silk Peasant may take the throne with a set.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
lot(6, "WB", [B(7, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 2, and only Silke bid: Graham is in debt and can't bid, and Paddy can't bid on his own lot. Silke's limit was 10. Paddy keeps a quarter, 0.5; 1.5 goes to the Harvest Pile.")), at="R13")
act("Nobody bids on Silke's Vase at 2, so it stays in Province 7's stock and must go up again next year. Nobody bids on the Harvest Pile's Land Deed Scroll or Customs House at 2; they stay in the pile's stock.", map_hl=[7])
swap(7, 5, "Silke completes the Wheat set (Jar, Customs House, Brush, Bowl) and replaces Graham as the Silk Peasant, in Province 5. She takes Graham's -19.5, leaving her 48, out of debt; the Dragon Jar goes back to Province 5's stock, and her Customs House, Brush and Bowl move to O20, O21 and O22. Graham draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Wheat Peasant, with Province 7 and its stock (the Vase and a Land Deed Scroll).",
 at={"CH": "O20", "BB": "O21", "WB": "O22"})
say("Silke", "Graham, you said a set wins nothing without a seat worth taking. The coast is worth taking. It's the only door to the throne at this table, and I've been on the wrong side of it for four years. You get my wheat and sixty clean grain from the Pile, and it's the best farm in China, so look after it.",
 "Silke: I drew the Silk seat at the start of this game, so Province 5 gets me back to even where the Wheat seat was a loss, and I can't think of a better way to spend nineteen and a half grain of Graham's debt. From the coast, a Land Deed Scroll and a Vase are all that stand between me and the crown, and one of those is sitting on the shelf I've just left.")
act("Salaries: the crown is still in debt, so as Reina said, the jobs' pay goes on the slate again, 3 a job: the crown now owes Paddy 12 (the tax and the I Ching, two years) and Silke 6 (the bank, two years), to be paid when it can. The jobs stay as they are: Paddy collects the tax and reads the I Ching; Silke banks the Harvest Pile.", "An appointee's pay is the Emperor's choice (Note 3); a crown in debt pays out nothing.", map_hl=[1,5,6])

act("The table gathers once more before Year 13's card is drawn. Before Paddy turns it, the four may agree to stop the game where it sits and score it (Note 19, Section 18); every player must agree, or play goes on into Year 13.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "I agree, and I won't pretend it's for the empire's sake. Stopping now hands me the win, and everyone here can count that as well as I can. Silke, that was beautifully done: the coast, forty-eight clean grain, three pieces of silk already on your shelf, and the Vase and a Scroll both sitting in the stock you just handed Graham. If you say no again, I'll understand exactly why, and I'll still turn up to the auction.",
 "Reina: She's now two pieces short of a Silk set, the Vase and a Scroll, with more than enough to claim, and both pieces have to go up cheap at next year's auction, so this vote is the last thing between her and my crown. All I can do is say yes, say it honestly, and keep Graham and Paddy remembering who sat beside them when the north went under.")
act("The Storyteller notes: Silke holds a Customs House, a Brush and the Bowl; a Silk set is the Land Deed Scroll, a Brush, the Bowl and the Vase, so she holds two of its four pieces and needs the Scroll and the Vase.", map_hl=[5])
act("The Storyteller notes to Silke: both the Vase and a Land Deed Scroll are in Province 7's stock, Graham's now; a second Land Deed Scroll is in the Harvest Pile's stock.", map_hl=[5,7])
say("Silke", "I said one more year, and then I'd be the one asking you to stop. I'm not asking, Reina, I'm agreeing. Take your win, you've earned it twice over. I finish on the coast I drew, with forty-eight grain I didn't have to borrow from anyone.",
 "Silke: Do the sum: the coast loses sixty-five in an ordinary year, and the harvest gets paid before the auctions, so by the time the Vase and Scroll came up at two I'd already be selling my pieces back to the Pile to cover it. I came back from Province 7 to the seat I started in, and that's a hold. I'll take it over one more card that could cost me everything.")
say("Paddy", "I agree. I started this game as the Rice Peasant and I'll finish it as the Rice Peasant, and I can't say that's nothing. Silke, here's the half grain the Bowl brought me. It's all I've got, and it's yours. The crown owes me twelve, and if it ever pays a grain of it, that grain goes straight to you until we're square. Reina, well played. Turning the cards for you has been an honour.",
 "Paddy: I'll go home owing Silke twenty-three and a half, which I hate more than losing the Deed. But I've handed over every grain I have, and a man can't do more than that.")
act("Paddy gives Silke his 0.5 against the 24 he promised her; 23.5 is still owed by promise.", "No deal is binding (Section 5).", {pid(6): -0.5, pid(5): 0.5}, map_hl=[5,6], deal=True)
say("Graham", "I agree. I drew Province 7, and I'm finishing in Province 7 with sixty clean grain and nothing owed to anyone. Silke, you took nineteen and a half of my debt to get that seat, and I've counted it. Reina, well played. The crown's books were never going to balance, and you knew it before any of us.",
 "Graham: From wheat I can't reach the throne, and the only two pieces that would send me back to the coast are sitting on Silke's shelf, so another year would only cost me grain I already have. Holding the province I drew, with the best surplus of any peasant, is the result the numbers give me, and I'll sign for it.")
act("Reina, Silke, Paddy and Graham all agree: the game stops here, at the end of Year 12, before Year 13's card is drawn (Note 19), and is scored as in Section 18. Every player sits in the province they drew: Reina keeps the throne, Silke the Silk seat, Paddy the Rice seat and Graham the Wheat seat. No grain changes hands for the agreement beyond Paddy's 0.5 to Silke.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7], big=True)
LAST_ROUND = 12   # the agreed stop (Note 19): the table agreed after Year 12's salaries, before Year 13's card

RESULT = export(
 "The table agreed after Year 12's salaries to stop where it sat (Note 19), with the crown 294.5 in debt and 200 of the 520 owed in public works built, so this is neither survival nor collapse. Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew; an Emperor still on the throne has won (Section 18).",
 ["Reina drew the throne. In Years 1–3 she built the Summer Palace, both Province 1 canal sections and the Province 7 Reservoir, then lost the throne to Silke in Year 3. She went from Silk to Wheat when Graham took her seat in Year 7, and completed a Wheat set to take the coast back in Year 8. In Year 10 she lent the crown 29 before the bad harvest, paid 53 for the last Scroll, exactly her grain less the 20 a claim needs, and took the throne back. The crown she held through the doubling of Year 11 fell 294.5 into debt. She said yes to stopping every time it was asked.",
  "Silke drew the Silk seat and took the throne in Year 3 with the Bowl and the Vase. As Empress she built the Winter Palace and the Marble Pagoda, and in Year 6 put the crown's reserve into land: thirteen squares in one season. Graham took the throne from her in Year 7, and Reina pushed her to the wheat in Year 8. She lent the crown 13 in Year 9 and priced the last Scroll at Reina's grain to the grain in Year 10. She said no to stopping twice before Year 11 and twice before Year 12, then bought the Wooden Bowl for 2, completed a Wheat set and went back to the Silk seat she drew, taking Graham's debt with it. Then she agreed.",
  "Paddy drew the Rice seat and kept it all game. He collected the tax and later read the I Ching, lost the Vase twice by a grain or so, and put almost every spare grain into land. In Year 7 he sold Graham the Dragon Jar he had held since the first auction. In Year 12 a buffalo disaster took his last pieces back to the Pile at 2 each. He finished with nothing, owing Silke 23.5 by promise.",
  "Graham drew the Wheat seat and paid 20 toward the Province 7 Reservoir, which made his the richest farm on the board. In Year 7 he bought Paddy's Jar, took the Silk seat from Reina and then the throne from Silke, three seats in one year. As Emperor for three years he borrowed before every bad harvest and kept the crown out of debt, but built nothing. Reina took the throne back in Year 10 and Silke the Silk seat in Year 12, and he finished in the province he drew with 60 grain and no debt.",
  "Land opened in Year 6, and the table bought 22 squares by Year 10. Province 1 had only 4 of them, and only the crown could buy its land. Two set-asides and a replay (in outtakes/) didn't change the count much. When the population doubled again in Year 11, the crown fell into debt and land shut for everyone. The Great Wall and the Grand Canal in Provinces 5 and 6, 320 between them, were never built."])
